-- Chemistry: syllabus gap-filling MCQs (CHE-6105..6135) and 200 difficult MCQs (CHE-7001..7200).
-- Gap questions: a fresh check of every PMDC MDCAT 2025 and NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry objective against the live bank
-- found a few objectives still with no or very few questions (photon as a unit of radiation energy, activated complex,
-- resonance energy, shape of ethene and its polymerisation, preparation and reactivity of alkynes, reactions of Group 1,
-- 2 and 13 elements with oxygen, chlorine, water and nitrogen, acid anhydrides and amides, classes of proteins).
-- Difficult questions: multi-step numericals, data and mechanism questions with close distractors, spread over every
-- MDCAT Chemistry chapter. Original questions in the bank's template; every key, distractor and explanation checked.
-- Own id blocks (6105 up and 7001 up), so the question counters are left alone. Not in the free set.
-- questions.chapter is the MDCAT chapter. NUMS rows only where the NUMS 2026 syllabus names the topic; every question is
-- in the AKU bank; FSc rows only where the PECTAA textbook chapter teaches the topic.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-6105', 'Atomic structure', 'Photon', 'easy', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.2', 'A photon is best described as', '["the smallest unit of electric charge", "a particle with the same rest mass as an electron", "a wave of light that carries no energy", "a discrete packet (quantum) of electromagnetic energy whose energy is E = hν"]', 3, 'Planck''s quantum theory says radiation is emitted or absorbed in discrete packets called quanta; a quantum of light is a photon, with energy E = hν, where h is Planck''s constant and ν the frequency.', '{"0": "That is the charge of an electron or proton, not a photon.", "1": "A photon has no rest mass.", "2": "Each photon carries energy hν."}'),
  ('CHE-6106', 'Atomic structure', 'Photon', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.2', 'What is the energy of one photon of light of wavelength 600 nm? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["3.3 × 10⁻²⁸ J", "1.1 × 10⁻²⁷ J", "2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J", "3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"]', 3, 'E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸) / (600 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ / 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', '{"0": "Dividing by 600 without converting nm to metres gives this.", "1": "This is h/λ (the photon''s momentum in kg m s⁻¹), not its energy.", "2": "This is hc alone; it has not been divided by λ."}'),
  ('CHE-6107', 'Atomic structure', 'Photon', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.2', 'Photon X has a wavelength of 400 nm and photon Y a wavelength of 800 nm. The ratio of their energies, E(X) : E(Y), is', '["2 : 1", "1 : 1", "4 : 1", "1 : 2"]', 0, 'E = hc/λ, so photon energy is inversely proportional to wavelength. Halving the wavelength doubles the energy: E(X) : E(Y) = 800 : 400 = 2 : 1.', '{"1": "Photons of different wavelength carry different energies.", "2": "Energy depends on 1/λ, not 1/λ².", "3": "This treats energy as directly proportional to wavelength."}'),
  ('CHE-6108', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Activated complex', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.5', 'The activated complex in a chemical reaction is', '["a stable intermediate that can be isolated and stored", "a compound of the catalyst and reactant that lowers ΔH of the reaction", "an unstable, highest-energy arrangement of atoms at the top of the energy barrier, in which old bonds are partly broken and new bonds partly formed", "the product formed in the fast step of a mechanism"]', 2, 'When colliding particles have at least the activation energy, they pass through a short-lived, high-energy arrangement (the activated complex or transition state) at the peak of the energy profile, which then either forms products or falls back to reactants.', '{"0": "It exists only for an instant at an energy maximum and cannot be isolated.", "1": "A catalyst lowers the activation energy, not ΔH, and the activated complex need not involve a catalyst.", "3": "Products are formed after the activated complex breaks up; they are not the complex itself."}'),
  ('CHE-6109', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Activated complex', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.6', 'On an energy profile, the reactants are at 50 kJ mol⁻¹, the activated complex at 170 kJ mol⁻¹ and the products at 20 kJ mol⁻¹. The activation energy of the reverse reaction is', '["30 kJ mol⁻¹", "120 kJ mol⁻¹", "150 kJ mol⁻¹", "170 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'The reverse reaction starts from the products: Ea(reverse) = 170 − 20 = 150 kJ mol⁻¹. (Ea forward = 170 − 50 = 120 kJ mol⁻¹, and ΔH forward = 20 − 50 = −30 kJ mol⁻¹.)', '{"0": "This is the size of ΔH, not an activation energy.", "1": "This is the activation energy of the forward reaction.", "3": "This is the energy of the activated complex itself, not the height of the barrier."}'),
  ('CHE-6110', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Activated complex', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.6', 'Two reactions are run at the same temperature with the same collision frequency. Reaction P has an activated complex 40 kJ mol⁻¹ above its reactants; reaction Q has one 90 kJ mol⁻¹ above its reactants. Which statement is correct?', '["Both have the same rate, because the collision frequency is the same", "P is faster, because a larger fraction of collisions have enough energy to reach its activated complex", "P is faster, because it must be more exothermic", "Q is faster, because a higher activated complex releases more energy"]', 1, 'Only collisions with energy at least equal to Ea form the activated complex. A lower barrier (40 kJ mol⁻¹) means a much larger fraction of molecules qualify, so P is faster.', '{"0": "Rate depends on the fraction of effective collisions, not just on how many collisions occur.", "2": "The activation energy says nothing about whether the reaction is exothermic.", "3": "A higher barrier means fewer effective collisions, so Q is slower."}'),
  ('CHE-6111', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Activated complex', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.5', 'Which statement correctly distinguishes an activated complex from a reaction intermediate in a two-step mechanism?', '["The intermediate lies at an energy maximum; the activated complex lies in an energy minimum", "The activated complex lies at an energy maximum and cannot be isolated; the intermediate lies in an energy minimum between the two steps", "They are the same species described in two different ways", "Both lie at energy maxima, but only the intermediate appears in the rate equation"]', 1, 'In a two-step profile there are two peaks (two activated complexes, one for each step) and a trough between them. The species in the trough is the intermediate: it has a real, if short, lifetime. Activated complexes sit at the peaks.', '{"0": "This reverses the two.", "2": "They are different: one is a transition arrangement at a peak, the other a real species in a trough.", "3": "The intermediate sits in a trough, not at a peak, and intermediates do not appear in the overall rate equation."}'),
  ('CHE-6112', 'Organic chemistry', 'Resonance energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.12', 'The resonance energy of benzene is', '["the difference between the energy of the hypothetical Kekulé structure (cyclohexatriene) and the actual, lower energy of benzene", "the energy difference between the two Kekulé structures of benzene", "the energy released when benzene is formed from cyclohexane", "the energy needed to break all the carbon–carbon bonds in benzene"]', 0, 'Resonance (delocalisation) energy is the extra stability of the real molecule compared with its most stable contributing structure. For benzene it is about 150 kJ mol⁻¹, found from enthalpies of hydrogenation.', '{"1": "The two Kekulé structures have the same energy, so their difference is zero.", "2": "Forming benzene from cyclohexane is a dehydrogenation; its enthalpy is not the resonance energy.", "3": "That is an atomisation-type energy, not resonance energy."}'),
  ('CHE-6113', 'Organic chemistry', 'Resonance energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.9; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.13', 'Which observation is best explained by the large resonance energy of benzene?', '["Benzene usually undergoes substitution rather than addition, so the delocalised ring is kept", "Benzene does not mix with water", "Benzene burns with a smoky, sooty flame", "Benzene has a lower boiling point than methylbenzene"]', 0, 'Addition would destroy the delocalised π system and lose the resonance stabilisation, so benzene prefers electrophilic substitution, which restores the aromatic ring in the product.', '{"1": "Insolubility is due to benzene being non-polar.", "2": "The sooty flame is due to the high carbon-to-hydrogen ratio.", "3": "Boiling points depend on intermolecular forces and molar mass, not on resonance energy."}'),
  ('CHE-6114', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structure of ethene', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.6', 'In ethene, the carbon–carbon π bond is formed by', '["overlap of an sp³ orbital with a 2p orbital", "sideways overlap of the unhybridised 2p orbitals on the two carbons, above and below the plane of the molecule", "head-on overlap of two sp² hybrid orbitals along the C–C axis", "overlap of the 1s orbitals of hydrogen with 2p orbitals of carbon"]', 1, 'Each carbon is sp² hybridised. The sp² orbitals form the σ framework (C–C and four C–H bonds), all in one plane with angles of about 120°. The leftover 2p orbitals, perpendicular to that plane, overlap sideways to form the π bond.', '{"0": "The carbons in ethene are sp², not sp³.", "2": "Head-on overlap of sp² orbitals gives the C–C σ bond, not the π bond.", "3": "H 1s orbitals overlap with carbon sp² orbitals to form σ C–H bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-6115', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structure of ethene', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.6', 'Why can the two CH₂ groups of ethene not rotate freely about the C=C bond at room temperature?', '["The σ bond between the carbons is too short to allow rotation", "The hydrogen atoms on the two carbons repel each other strongly", "Each carbon is sp³ hybridised and locked in a tetrahedral shape", "Rotation would twist the parallel p orbitals apart and break the π bond"]', 3, 'The π bond exists only while the two p orbitals stay parallel. Turning one end through 90° would destroy the overlap, which needs about 250 kJ mol⁻¹, so rotation does not happen at room temperature. This is why alkenes can show cis-trans isomerism.', '{"0": "Rotation about a σ bond alone is free, whatever its length.", "1": "Repulsion between H atoms is small and is not what blocks rotation.", "2": "The carbons in ethene are sp² hybridised and planar."}'),
  ('CHE-6116', 'Organic chemistry', 'Polymerisation of ethene', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.10', 'A polyethene chain is made by joining 1000 ethene molecules (C₂H₄ = 28 g mol⁻¹) by addition polymerisation. Ignoring the end groups, its molar mass is about', '["14 000 g mol⁻¹", "26 000 g mol⁻¹", "27 982 g mol⁻¹", "28 000 g mol⁻¹"]', 3, 'In addition polymerisation nothing is lost: the C=C bonds open and the monomers join, so the polymer''s mass is n × 28 = 1000 × 28 = 28 000 g mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses CH₂ (14) as if it were the monomer.", "1": "This subtracts 2 g mol⁻¹ per monomer as if hydrogen were lost; no atoms are lost in addition polymerisation.", "2": "This subtracts 18 g mol⁻¹ as if water were lost; that happens only in condensation polymerisation."}'),
  ('CHE-6117', 'Organic chemistry', 'Preparation of alkynes', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.16; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.20', '1,2-Dibromoethane is heated with an excess of a strong base such as sodium amide (or hot alcoholic KOH). The main organic product is', '["ethane-1,2-diol", "bromoethene only", "ethyne", "ethene"]', 2, 'Two molecules of HBr are removed in two elimination steps: BrCH₂CH₂Br → CH₂=CHBr → HC≡CH. A strong base in excess drives the second, harder elimination.', '{"0": "Substitution by OH⁻ to give a diol happens in aqueous base, not with sodium amide or hot alcoholic KOH.", "1": "Bromoethene is formed after the first elimination, but excess strong base removes the second HBr too.", "3": "Ethene would need removal of Br₂ (for example with zinc), not HBr."}'),
  ('CHE-6118', 'Organic chemistry', 'Preparation of alkynes', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.16; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.20', 'Ethyne is made in the laboratory by adding water to calcium carbide: CaC₂ + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + C₂H₂. What volume of ethyne at STP can be made from 6.4 g of CaC₂? (CaC₂ = 64)', '["1.12 dm³", "2.24 dm³", "4.48 dm³", "22.4 dm³"]', 1, 'CaC₂ = 6.4/64 = 0.10 mol, which gives 0.10 mol C₂H₂ = 0.10 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³.', '{"0": "This halves the moles.", "2": "This doubles the moles, as if both water molecules gave ethyne.", "3": "This is the volume of one whole mole."}'),
  ('CHE-6119', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactivity of alkynes', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.19; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.10', 'Which order of reactivity towards electrophilic addition of bromine (in the dark, no catalyst) is generally correct?', '["ethene > ethyne > benzene", "ethane > ethene > ethyne", "benzene > ethene > ethyne", "ethyne > ethene > benzene"]', 0, 'Alkenes add bromine fastest. Alkynes also add, but more slowly, because the π electrons of the sp carbons are held more tightly and the vinyl-type carbocation formed is less stable. Benzene does not add bromine under these conditions because addition would destroy its aromatic stability.', '{"1": "Ethane is saturated and cannot undergo addition at all.", "2": "Benzene is the least reactive towards addition.", "3": "Although ethyne has two π bonds, it is less reactive than ethene towards electrophiles."}'),
  ('CHE-6120', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 2 elements', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.4', 'When magnesium ribbon burns in air, the white ash gives a smell of ammonia when water is added. This is because', '["magnesium nitrate is formed, which decomposes in water to ammonia", "magnesium hydride is formed and reacts with nitrogen from the water", "some magnesium reacts with nitrogen to form magnesium nitride, Mg₃N₂, which reacts with water to release NH₃", "magnesium oxide reacts with water to release ammonia"]', 2, 'Mg is reactive enough to combine with N₂ when it burns in air: 3Mg + N₂ → Mg₃N₂. The nitride is hydrolysed: Mg₃N₂ + 6H₂O → 3Mg(OH)₂ + 2NH₃.', '{"0": "Nitrate is not formed when magnesium burns.", "1": "Water contains no nitrogen, and burning magnesium does not form a hydride.", "3": "MgO contains no nitrogen, so it cannot give ammonia."}'),
  ('CHE-6121', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 2 elements', 'easy', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.4', 'Calcium heated in nitrogen gas forms calcium nitride. Its formula is', '["Ca(N₃)₂", "CaN", "Ca₂N₃", "Ca₃N₂"]', 3, 'Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and nitride is N³⁻. Balancing the charges: 3 × (+2) = +6 and 2 × (−3) = −6, so the formula is Ca₃N₂ (3Ca + N₂ → Ca₃N₂).', '{"0": "This is calcium azide (N₃⁻), not the nitride.", "1": "CaN would need Ca³⁺ or N²⁻.", "2": "This swaps the numbers and does not balance the charges."}'),
  ('CHE-6122', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 1 elements', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.3', 'Which alkali metal reacts directly with nitrogen, even at room temperature, to form a nitride?', '["Potassium", "Lithium", "Caesium", "Sodium"]', 1, 'Lithium forms Li₃N (6Li + N₂ → 2Li₃N). The very small Li⁺ ion gives a strong lattice with the small N³⁻ ion, one of the ways lithium resembles magnesium (diagonal relationship). The other alkali metals do not form nitrides this way.', '{"0": "Potassium does not form a nitride directly.", "2": "Caesium is the most reactive with water but does not form a nitride directly.", "3": "Sodium does not react with nitrogen under ordinary conditions."}'),
  ('CHE-6123', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 1 elements', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.3', 'When burned in excess oxygen, which pairing of metal and main product is correct?', '["Potassium – K₂O (normal oxide)", "Sodium – NaO₂ (superoxide)", "Potassium – KO₂ (superoxide)", "Lithium – Li₂O₂ (peroxide)"]', 2, 'In excess oxygen, Li gives mainly the oxide Li₂O, Na mainly the peroxide Na₂O₂, and K, Rb and Cs the superoxides (KO₂). Larger cations can stabilise the larger peroxide and superoxide ions.', '{"0": "Potassium in excess oxygen goes beyond the normal oxide to KO₂.", "1": "Sodium gives mainly Na₂O₂.", "3": "Lithium gives mainly Li₂O."}'),
  ('CHE-6124', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 1 elements', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.3', '4.6 g of sodium burns completely in chlorine. What mass of sodium chloride is formed? (Na = 23, Cl = 35.5)', '["5.85 g", "8.15 g", "11.7 g", "23.4 g"]', 2, '2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl. Moles of Na = 4.6 / 23 = 0.20 mol, giving 0.20 mol NaCl = 0.20 × 58.5 = 11.7 g.', '{"0": "This is the mass from 0.10 mol, as if two Na gave one NaCl.", "1": "This adds the masses of 4.6 g Na and 3.55 g Cl, using only half the chlorine needed.", "3": "This doubles the moles of NaCl."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-6125', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 1 elements', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.3', 'The reaction of the Group 1 metals with cold water becomes more vigorous from lithium to caesium mainly because', '["the first ionisation energy decreases down the group, so the outer electron is lost more easily", "the melting point increases down the group", "the hydration energy of the ions increases down the group", "the hydroxides become less soluble down the group"]', 0, 'Down the group the outer s electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so less energy is needed to remove it and the metal reacts faster.', '{"1": "Melting points decrease down Group 1.", "2": "Hydration energy becomes less negative down the group, because the ions get larger.", "3": "Group 1 hydroxides are all soluble, and solubility is not the reason."}'),
  ('CHE-6126', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 13 elements', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.5', 'Aluminium is a reactive metal, yet an aluminium pot does not react with water. The main reason is', '["aluminium forms a soluble hydroxide that is washed away", "aluminium is a non-metal", "aluminium is below hydrogen in the electrochemical series", "a thin, tough, unreactive layer of aluminium oxide covers the metal and protects it"]', 3, 'Aluminium (E° = −1.66 V) should react with water, but the surface Al₂O₃ layer is impervious and sticks firmly, so the reaction stops before it starts. If the layer is removed (for example with mercury salts), the metal does react with water.', '{"0": "Al(OH)₃ is insoluble in water.", "1": "Aluminium is a metal.", "2": "Aluminium is well above hydrogen (E° = −1.66 V)."}'),
  ('CHE-6127', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 13 elements', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.5', 'In the vapour at moderate temperatures, aluminium chloride exists as Al₂Cl₆ molecules. This is because', '["aluminium forms Al–Al bonds in the vapour", "the aluminium in AlCl₃ has only six outer electrons, so a lone pair from a chlorine of another AlCl₃ forms a coordinate bond to it", "aluminium chloride is an ionic solid that keeps its lattice in the vapour", "the chlorine atoms form Cl–Cl bonds between two AlCl₃ units"]', 1, 'AlCl₃ is electron-deficient. Two molecules join through two bridging Cl atoms, each donating a lone pair to the other Al, so each Al reaches an octet and becomes roughly tetrahedral.', '{"0": "The dimer is held by Al–Cl–Al bridges, not by an Al–Al bond.", "2": "Aluminium chloride is largely covalent; it sublimes as molecules, not as an ionic lattice.", "3": "There are no Cl–Cl bonds in Al₂Cl₆."}'),
  ('CHE-6128', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Acid anhydrides', 'easy', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Ethanoic anhydride, (CH₃CO)₂O, reacts with water to give', '["two molecules of ethanoic acid", "ethyl ethanoate", "ethanoic acid and ethanol", "ethanal and ethanoic acid"]', 0, 'Hydrolysis breaks the C–O–C link between the two acyl groups: (CH₃CO)₂O + H₂O → 2CH₃COOH.', '{"1": "An ester forms when the anhydride reacts with ethanol, not with water.", "2": "No alcohol is formed; the anhydride has no ethyl group.", "3": "No reduction occurs, so no aldehyde forms."}'),
  ('CHE-6129', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Acid derivatives', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Which order shows the reactivity of carboxylic acid derivatives towards nucleophilic acyl substitution (for example hydrolysis)?', '["acyl chloride > acid anhydride > ester > amide", "acid anhydride > acyl chloride > amide > ester", "amide > ester > acid anhydride > acyl chloride", "ester > acyl chloride > amide > acid anhydride"]', 0, 'The better the leaving group, the more reactive the derivative: Cl⁻ is the best, then RCOO⁻, then RO⁻, and NH₂⁻ is the worst. Acyl chlorides fume in moist air; amides need prolonged heating with acid or alkali.', '{"1": "Acyl chlorides are more reactive than anhydrides, and esters more than amides.", "2": "This is the reverse order.", "3": "Acyl chlorides are the most reactive, not esters."}'),
  ('CHE-6130', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Amides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Organic Nitrogen Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl, reacts with excess concentrated ammonia. The products are', '["ethanoic acid and ammonium chloride", "ethylamine and hydrogen chloride", "ethanenitrile and water", "ethanamide and ammonium chloride"]', 3, 'NH₃ attacks the carbonyl carbon and Cl⁻ is lost: CH₃COCl + 2NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + NH₄Cl. The second NH₃ neutralises the HCl formed.', '{"0": "Ethanoic acid would come from water, not ammonia.", "1": "The C=O group is kept, so an amide (not an amine) forms, and excess ammonia takes up the HCl.", "2": "A nitrile needs dehydration of the amide, for example with P₄O₁₀."}'),
  ('CHE-6131', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Acid anhydrides', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Which reaction is a laboratory preparation of ethanoic anhydride?', '["Ethanal + acidified potassium dichromate", "Ethanoic acid + sodium hydroxide", "Ethanoyl chloride + sodium ethanoate", "Ethanol + ethanoic acid with a little concentrated H₂SO₄"]', 2, 'The ethanoate ion acts as the nucleophile and replaces Cl: CH₃COCl + CH₃COONa → (CH₃CO)₂O + NaCl.', '{"0": "This oxidises ethanal to ethanoic acid.", "1": "This is neutralisation; it gives sodium ethanoate and water.", "3": "This is esterification; it gives ethyl ethanoate."}'),
  ('CHE-6132', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Amides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Organic Nitrogen Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Ethanamide is boiled with dilute hydrochloric acid. The products are', '["ethanenitrile and water", "ethanoic acid and ammonium chloride", "ethanoyl chloride and ammonia", "ethylamine and water"]', 1, 'Acid hydrolysis of an amide: CH₃CONH₂ + H₂O + HCl → CH₃COOH + NH₄Cl. In acid the ammonia released is protonated to NH₄⁺.', '{"0": "Losing water to form a nitrile needs a dehydrating agent, not dilute acid.", "2": "Acyl chlorides are more reactive than amides, so hydrolysis does not form them.", "3": "Reduction (for example with LiAlH₄) is needed to make an amine from an amide."}'),
  ('CHE-6133', 'Macromolecules', 'Classification of proteins', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'Haemoglobin is made of polypeptide chains attached to a non-protein haem group containing iron. It is therefore classed as a', '["derived protein", "simple protein", "conjugated protein", "fibrous protein"]', 2, 'Conjugated proteins have a non-protein (prosthetic) group joined to the protein part, such as haem in haemoglobin or carbohydrate in glycoproteins.', '{"0": "Derived proteins are breakdown products of proteins, such as peptones.", "1": "Simple proteins give only amino acids on hydrolysis.", "3": "Haemoglobin is a soluble globular protein, not a fibrous one."}'),
  ('CHE-6134', 'Macromolecules', 'Classification of proteins', 'moderate', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'Derived proteins are best described as', '["substances formed by partial hydrolysis or denaturation of natural proteins, such as proteoses and peptones", "proteins made only by plants", "proteins that give only amino acids on complete hydrolysis", "proteins that contain a metal ion as a prosthetic group"]', 0, 'Proteins are classified as simple (only amino acids on hydrolysis), conjugated (protein plus a prosthetic group) and derived (products of breaking down natural proteins, such as proteoses, peptones and peptides).', '{"1": "The class is based on composition, not on whether a plant makes it.", "2": "That describes simple proteins such as albumin.", "3": "That describes a conjugated metalloprotein."}'),
  ('CHE-6135', 'Macromolecules', 'Classification of proteins', 'easy', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'Complete hydrolysis of a simple protein such as albumin gives', '["glucose and amino acids", "amino acids and a haem group", "fatty acids and glycerol", "only α-amino acids"]', 3, 'Simple proteins are made of amino acid units only, so complete hydrolysis of their peptide bonds gives only amino acids.', '{"0": "Carbohydrate in the products would point to a glycoprotein (conjugated).", "1": "A haem group comes from a conjugated protein such as haemoglobin.", "2": "Fatty acids and glycerol come from lipids."}'),
  ('CHE-7001', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.4', '10.0 g of hydrogen is exploded with 64.0 g of oxygen to form water. What is left over when the reaction is complete? (H = 1, O = 16)', '["16 g of oxygen", "2.0 g of hydrogen", "1.0 g of hydrogen", "Nothing; both reactants are used up"]', 1, '2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. H₂ = 10.0/2 = 5.0 mol; O₂ = 64.0/32 = 2.0 mol. 2.0 mol O₂ needs only 4.0 mol H₂, so O₂ is limiting and 1.0 mol H₂ (2.0 g) is left; 72 g of water forms.', '{"0": "Oxygen is the limiting reactant, so none of it is left.", "2": "1.0 g is the mass of 0.5 mol H₂; the excess is 1.0 mol, which is 2.0 g.", "3": "The reactants are not in the 2 : 1 mole ratio (5 : 2), so one must be in excess."}'),
  ('CHE-7002', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage yield', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.5; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.6', '25.0 g of limestone that is 80% CaCO₃ by mass is heated strongly, and 9.52 g of CaO is obtained. What is the percentage yield? (CaCO₃ = 100, CaO = 56)', '["47.6%", "68%", "80%", "85%"]', 3, 'Pure CaCO₃ = 0.80 × 25.0 = 20.0 g = 0.200 mol, so the theoretical CaO = 0.200 × 56 = 11.2 g. Yield = 9.52 / 11.2 × 100 = 85%.', '{"0": "This compares the mass of CaO with the mass of CaCO₃ (9.52/20.0), but they have different molar masses.", "1": "This uses all 25.0 g as CaCO₃ (theoretical 14.0 g), ignoring the 80% purity.", "2": "This is the purity of the limestone, not the yield."}'),
  ('CHE-7003', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Stoichiometry with gas volumes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', '11.0 g of propane, C₃H₈, burns completely in excess oxygen. What volume of oxygen at STP is used up? (C = 12, H = 1)', '["5.6 dm³", "16.8 dm³", "22.4 dm³", "28.0 dm³"]', 3, 'C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. Propane = 11.0/44 = 0.25 mol, so O₂ = 5 × 0.25 = 1.25 mol = 1.25 × 22.4 = 28.0 dm³.', '{"0": "This is the volume of the propane itself.", "1": "This is the volume of CO₂ formed (0.75 mol).", "2": "This is 1.0 mol, the amount of water formed; water is not a gas at STP anyway."}'),
  ('CHE-7004', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole and Avogadro''s number', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', 'How many atoms in total are present in 4.4 g of carbon dioxide? (C = 12, O = 16, NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)', '["6.02 × 10²²", "1.20 × 10²³", "1.81 × 10²³", "7.95 × 10²⁴"]', 2, 'CO₂ = 4.4/44 = 0.10 mol of molecules. Each molecule has 3 atoms, so atoms = 0.30 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.81 × 10²³.', '{"0": "This is the number of molecules, not atoms.", "1": "This counts only the oxygen atoms.", "3": "This uses 4.4 as the number of moles instead of the mass in grams."}'),
  ('CHE-7005', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole and Avogadro''s number', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', '0.20 mol of aluminium sulphate, Al₂(SO₄)₃, is dissolved in water and dissociates completely. How many ions are present in the solution?', '["1.20 × 10²³", "2.41 × 10²³", "3.61 × 10²³", "6.02 × 10²³"]', 3, 'Al₂(SO₄)₃ → 2Al³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻, giving 5 ions per formula unit. Ions = 5 × 0.20 = 1.0 mol = 6.02 × 10²³.', '{"0": "This is the number of formula units dissolved (0.20 mol).", "1": "This counts only the Al³⁺ ions (0.40 mol).", "2": "This counts only the sulphate ions (0.60 mol)."}'),
  ('CHE-7006', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole ratios', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.1', 'Nitric oxide is made from hydrogen in two steps: N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, then 4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O. Assuming complete conversion, how many moles of H₂ are needed to make 2 mol of NO?', '["1.5 mol", "2 mol", "3 mol", "6 mol"]', 2, '2 mol NO needs 2 mol NH₃ (4 : 4). 2 mol NH₃ needs 3 mol H₂ (2 : 3). So 3 mol H₂.', '{"0": "This halves the amount needed.", "1": "This assumes one H₂ per NO.", "3": "This doubles the amount: 6 mol H₂ would give 4 mol NH₃ and 4 mol NO."}'),
  ('CHE-7007', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.4', '5.4 g of aluminium is heated in 21.3 g of chlorine: 2Al + 3Cl₂ → 2AlCl₃. Which statement is correct? (Al = 27, Cl = 35.5)', '["13.4 g of AlCl₃ forms and chlorine is the limiting reactant", "26.7 g of AlCl₃ forms and neither reactant is in excess", "26.7 g of AlCl₃ forms and chlorine is in excess", "40.1 g of AlCl₃ forms and aluminium is in excess"]', 1, 'Al = 5.4/27 = 0.20 mol; Cl₂ = 21.3/71 = 0.30 mol. The ratio 0.20 : 0.30 is exactly 2 : 3, so both are used up and 0.20 mol AlCl₃ (0.20 × 133.5 = 26.7 g) forms.', '{"0": "This halves the product; the reactants are in exact proportion, so neither limits the other.", "2": "0.30 mol Cl₂ is exactly what 0.20 mol Al needs, so none is left.", "3": "This uses 0.30 mol AlCl₃, as if Cl₂ and AlCl₃ were in a 1 : 1 ratio."}'),
  ('CHE-7008', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Empirical and molecular formula', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', '0.30 g of a compound of C, H and O burns completely to give 0.44 g of CO₂ and 0.18 g of H₂O. Its molar mass is 60 g mol⁻¹. Its molecular formula is', '["C₂H₄O₂", "C₂H₆O", "CH₂O", "C₃H₈O"]', 0, 'C = 0.44 × 12/44 = 0.12 g (0.010 mol); H = 0.18 × 2/18 = 0.020 g (0.020 mol); O = 0.30 − 0.12 − 0.02 = 0.16 g (0.010 mol). Empirical formula CH₂O (30), and 60/30 = 2, so C₂H₄O₂.', '{"1": "This has molar mass 46 and the wrong C : H : O ratio.", "2": "This is only the empirical formula; its mass is 30, not 60.", "3": "This also has molar mass 60 but does not fit the C : H : O ratio of 1 : 2 : 1."}'),
  ('CHE-7009', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage yield', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.5; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.6', 'Ethyl ethanoate is made by CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O, with a yield of 50%. What mass of ethanoic acid is needed to obtain 22.0 g of ester? (CH₃COOH = 60, CH₃COOC₂H₅ = 88)', '["15 g", "30 g", "44 g", "60 g"]', 1, 'Actual ester = 22.0/88 = 0.25 mol. At 50% yield the theoretical amount must be 0.50 mol, so 0.50 mol of acid = 0.50 × 60 = 30 g.', '{"0": "This ignores the 50% yield.", "2": "This is the theoretical mass of ester (0.50 × 88), not of the acid.", "3": "This applies the yield twice (1.0 mol)."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7010', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole and Avogadro''s number', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', 'Which sample contains the greatest number of atoms? (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16)', '["0.20 g of H₂", "1.6 g of CH₄", "2.24 dm³ of NH₃ at STP", "3.2 g of O₂"]', 1, 'Each sample is 0.10 mol of molecules, so the atom count depends on atoms per molecule: CH₄ 0.10 × 5 = 0.50 mol atoms; NH₃ 0.40; O₂ 0.20; H₂ 0.20.', '{"0": "0.10 mol H₂ has only 0.20 mol atoms.", "2": "0.10 mol NH₃ has 0.40 mol atoms, fewer than CH₄.", "3": "0.10 mol O₂ has only 0.20 mol atoms."}'),
  ('CHE-7011', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.4', '6.5 g of zinc is added to 100 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. What volume of hydrogen forms at STP? (Zn = 65)', '["0.56 dm³", "1.12 dm³", "2.24 dm³", "4.48 dm³"]', 1, 'Zn = 0.10 mol; HCl = 0.100 dm³ × 1.0 = 0.10 mol. 0.10 mol Zn would need 0.20 mol HCl, so HCl is limiting. H₂ = 0.10/2 = 0.050 mol = 1.12 dm³ (and 3.25 g of zinc is left).', '{"0": "This halves the moles of H₂ a second time.", "2": "This assumes zinc is limiting.", "3": "This doubles the amount from zinc."}'),
  ('CHE-7012', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage composition', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', 'Which fertiliser contains the highest percentage of nitrogen by mass? (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, S = 32, Cl = 35.5)', '["Urea, CO(NH₂)₂", "Ammonium sulphate, (NH₄)₂SO₄", "Ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃", "Ammonium chloride, NH₄Cl"]', 0, '%N: urea 28/60 = 46.7%; NH₄NO₃ 28/80 = 35.0%; (NH₄)₂SO₄ 28/132 = 21.2%; NH₄Cl 14/53.5 = 26.2%.', '{"1": "21.2%, the lowest of the four.", "2": "35.0%, less than urea.", "3": "26.2%."}'),
  ('CHE-7013', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Stoichiometry with gas volumes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.1; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', '10 cm³ of a gaseous hydrocarbon needs exactly 50 cm³ of oxygen for complete combustion and produces 30 cm³ of carbon dioxide (all volumes at the same temperature and pressure). The hydrocarbon is', '["C₃H₄", "C₂H₆", "C₃H₈", "C₃H₆"]', 2, 'CₓHᵧ + (x + y/4)O₂ → xCO₂ + (y/2)H₂O. Volumes are in mole ratio: CO₂ 30/10 gives x = 3; O₂ 50/10 = 5 = 3 + y/4 gives y = 8. So C₃H₈.', '{"0": "C₃H₄ needs 4 volumes of O₂ (40 cm³).", "1": "C₂H₆ gives only 20 cm³ of CO₂.", "3": "C₃H₆ needs 4.5 volumes of O₂ (45 cm³)."}'),
  ('CHE-7014', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.4', '50 cm³ of 0.20 mol dm⁻³ AgNO₃ is mixed with 50 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ NaCl. What mass of AgCl precipitates? (AgCl = 143.5)', '["0.36 g", "0.72 g", "1.08 g", "1.44 g"]', 1, 'Ag⁺ = 0.050 × 0.20 = 0.010 mol; Cl⁻ = 0.050 × 0.10 = 0.0050 mol. Cl⁻ is limiting, so AgCl = 0.0050 mol = 0.0050 × 143.5 = 0.72 g.', '{"0": "This halves the limiting amount.", "2": "This uses 0.0075 mol, the average of the two amounts.", "3": "This assumes all the silver precipitates; there is not enough chloride."}'),
  ('CHE-7015', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole and Avogadro''s number', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.2', 'How many water molecules are in one drop of water of volume 0.050 cm³? (density 1.0 g cm⁻³, H₂O = 18, NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)', '["2.8 × 10⁻³", "1.7 × 10²¹", "3.0 × 10²²", "5.4 × 10²³"]', 1, 'Mass = 0.050 g. Moles = 0.050/18 = 2.8 × 10⁻³ mol. Molecules = 2.8 × 10⁻³ × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.7 × 10²¹.', '{"0": "This is the number of moles, not molecules.", "2": "This treats 0.050 g as 0.050 mol.", "3": "This multiplies by the molar mass instead of dividing."}'),
  ('CHE-7016', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage yield', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.5; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 1.6', 'A drug is made in two steps, A → B (80% yield) and then B → C (75% yield), with 1 : 1 mole ratios. How many moles of C are obtained from 0.50 mol of A?', '["0.30 mol", "0.375 mol", "0.39 mol", "0.40 mol"]', 0, 'Yields multiply: B = 0.50 × 0.80 = 0.40 mol; C = 0.40 × 0.75 = 0.30 mol. The overall yield is 60%.', '{"1": "This applies only the second step''s yield.", "2": "This averages the two yields (77.5%); yields of successive steps multiply.", "3": "This is the amount of B, after the first step only."}'),
  ('CHE-7017', 'Atomic structure', 'Quantum numbers', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.4', 'How many electrons in a single atom can have the quantum numbers n = 3 and m = −1?', '["2", "4", "6", "8"]', 1, 'For n = 3, l can be 0, 1 or 2. m = −1 is possible only for l = 1 (3p) and l = 2 (3d), so there are two such orbitals, each holding 2 electrons: 4 electrons.', '{"0": "This counts only one orbital (3p or 3d).", "2": "This counts all three 3p orbitals instead of only those with m = −1.", "3": "This counts orbitals with m = +1 as well as m = −1 (four orbitals)."}'),
  ('CHE-7018', 'Atomic structure', 'Quantum numbers', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.4', 'Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, m, s) is NOT allowed for an electron?', '["2, 1, 0, −½", "4, 0, 0, +½", "3, 2, −2, +½", "2, 2, 1, +½"]', 3, 'l can only take values from 0 to n − 1. For n = 2, l can be 0 or 1, so l = 2 is impossible (there is no 2d subshell).', '{"0": "Allowed: a 2p electron.", "1": "Allowed: a 4s electron.", "2": "Allowed: a 3d electron (l = 2, m = −2)."}'),
  ('CHE-7019', 'Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.7; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.8', 'Which species has the greatest number of unpaired electrons? (Cr = 24, Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Co = 27)', '["Co atom", "Mn²⁺ ion", "Cr atom", "Fe³⁺ ion"]', 2, 'Cr is [Ar]3d⁵4s¹, with 6 unpaired electrons (half-filled 3d and 4s are more stable). Fe³⁺ and Mn²⁺ are both [Ar]3d⁵ (5 unpaired); Co is [Ar]3d⁷4s² (3 unpaired).', '{"0": "Co has 3 unpaired electrons.", "1": "Mn²⁺ has 5 unpaired electrons.", "3": "Fe³⁺ has 5 unpaired electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-7020', 'Atomic structure', 'Hydrogen spectrum and Bohr model', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.6', 'In the Bohr model the energy of the hydrogen electron is Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV. How much energy is needed to remove the electron completely from a hydrogen atom that is in the n = 2 state?', '["1.51 eV", "3.4 eV", "10.2 eV", "13.6 eV"]', 1, 'Ionisation takes the electron from n = 2 to n = ∞ (E = 0). E₂ = −13.6/4 = −3.4 eV, so 3.4 eV is needed.', '{"0": "This is the size of E₃, the n = 3 level.", "2": "This is the energy of the n = 1 → n = 2 jump.", "3": "This is the ionisation energy from the ground state (n = 1)."}'),
  ('CHE-7021', 'Atomic structure', 'Hydrogen spectrum and Bohr model', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.6', 'Electrons in a sample of hydrogen atoms are excited to n = 4. As they return to n = 1 by every possible route, how many different spectral lines can be emitted?', '["3", "4", "6", "10"]', 2, 'Lines correspond to every pair of levels among n = 1 to 4: 4→3, 4→2, 4→1, 3→2, 3→1, 2→1, which is n(n − 1)/2 = 4 × 3/2 = 6.', '{"0": "This counts only one-step drops (4→3, 3→2, 2→1).", "1": "This counts the number of levels, not the transitions between them.", "3": "This is the count for n = 5."}'),
  ('CHE-7022', 'Atomic structure', 'Aufbau principle', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.7', 'Using the (n + l) rule, which order of filling is correct?', '["4s < 3d < 4p < 5s", "4s < 4p < 3d < 5s", "3d < 4s < 4p < 5s", "4s < 3d < 5s < 4p"]', 0, '(n + l) values: 4s = 4, 3d = 5, 4p = 5, 5s = 5. The lowest sum fills first; for equal sums the subshell with lower n fills first, so 3d (n = 3) < 4p (n = 4) < 5s (n = 5).', '{"1": "3d and 4p have the same sum, so 3d (lower n) fills first.", "2": "4s (n + l = 4) fills before 3d (5).", "3": "4p (n = 4) fills before 5s (n = 5)."}'),
  ('CHE-7023', 'Atomic structure', 'Hydrogen spectrum and Bohr model', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.6', 'Which electron transition in a hydrogen atom emits the photon of highest energy? (Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV)', '["n = 3 → n = 2", "n = 6 → n = 2", "n = ∞ → n = 2", "n = 2 → n = 1"]', 3, 'ΔE: 2→1 = 13.6 × (1 − ¼) = 10.2 eV; ∞→2 = 3.4 eV; 6→2 = 13.6 × (¼ − 1/36) = 3.0 eV; 3→2 = 1.9 eV. Jumps to n = 1 release the most energy because the levels are furthest apart near the nucleus.', '{"0": "About 1.9 eV (the red Balmer line).", "1": "About 3.0 eV.", "2": "Only 3.4 eV: even the longest drop into n = 2 releases less than 2→1."}'),
  ('CHE-7024', 'Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.7; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.8', 'How many electrons occupy p orbitals in a sulphur atom (Z = 16)?', '["4", "6", "10", "16"]', 2, 'S: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴. p electrons = 6 (2p) + 4 (3p) = 10.', '{"0": "This counts only the 3p electrons.", "1": "This counts only the 2p electrons.", "3": "This is the total number of electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-7025', 'Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.8', 'The electronic configuration of the Cu⁺ ion (Cu, Z = 29) is', '["[Ar] 3d¹⁰", "[Ar] 3d⁹ 4s¹", "[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹", "[Ar] 3d⁸ 4s²"]', 0, 'Copper is [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹ (a full 3d subshell is extra stable). When it forms Cu⁺, the 4s electron is lost first, leaving [Ar]3d¹⁰.', '{"1": "This removes the electron from 3d instead of 4s.", "2": "This is the neutral copper atom.", "3": "This is neither copper''s configuration nor its ion''s."}'),
  ('CHE-7026', 'Atomic structure', 'Positive rays', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 2.1', 'Positive rays are produced in discharge tubes filled with different gases. For which gas do the positive rays have the largest charge-to-mass ratio (e/m)?', '["Hydrogen", "Neon", "Helium", "Oxygen"]', 0, 'Positive rays are ions of the gas, so e/m depends on the gas. The lightest positive ion, H⁺ (a proton), has the largest e/m; this is how the proton was identified.', '{"1": "Ne⁺ is much heavier than H⁺.", "2": "He⁺ has about four times the mass of H⁺ for the same charge.", "3": "O⁺ is much heavier than H⁺."}'),
  ('CHE-7027', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionic radius', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.9', 'Which order of ionic radius is correct for these isoelectronic ions?', '["Na⁺ > Mg²⁺ > O²⁻ > F⁻", "F⁻ > O²⁻ > Mg²⁺ > Na⁺", "Mg²⁺ > Na⁺ > F⁻ > O²⁻", "O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺"]', 3, 'All four have 10 electrons. The more protons, the stronger the pull on the same electron cloud: O (8 protons) gives the largest ion and Mg (12 protons) the smallest.', '{"0": "Cations here are smaller than the anions, because they have more protons for the same 10 electrons.", "1": "O²⁻ has fewer protons than F⁻, so it is larger; Na⁺ is larger than Mg²⁺.", "2": "This is the reverse order."}'),
  ('CHE-7028', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionisation energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Which statement about first ionisation energies in Period 3 is correct?', '["Mg > Al and P > S", "Al > Mg and S > P", "Mg > Al and S > P", "Al > Mg and P > S"]', 0, 'Al''s electron is removed from a 3p orbital, which is higher in energy and shielded by 3s², so Al < Mg. In S, the electron is removed from a doubly occupied 3p orbital, where paired electrons repel, so S < P.', '{"1": "Both are the reverse of the real dips.", "2": "S is lower than P.", "3": "Al is lower than Mg."}'),
  ('CHE-7029', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionisation energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.9', 'An element has successive ionisation energies of 578, 1817, 2745 and 11 577 kJ mol⁻¹. In which group of the periodic table is it?', '["Group 1", "Group 14", "Group 13", "Group 2"]', 2, 'The big jump comes after the third electron (2745 → 11 577), so three electrons are in the outer shell and the fourth comes from an inner shell. The element is in Group 13 (these are aluminium''s values).', '{"0": "A Group 1 element would show the big jump after the first electron.", "1": "A Group 14 element would show it after the fourth.", "3": "A Group 2 element would show it after the second."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7030', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.9', 'Chlorine releases more energy than fluorine when it gains an electron, although fluorine is more electronegative. The reason is that', '["fluorine already has a full outer shell", "chlorine''s added electron enters a 3d orbital", "chlorine has a higher nuclear charge, which outweighs everything else", "fluorine is so small that the added electron is strongly repelled by the electrons already crowded in its 2p orbitals"]', 3, 'The extra electron enters the compact n = 2 shell of fluorine, where electron–electron repulsion is large; in the larger chlorine atom the electron density is spread out, so more energy is released overall.', '{"0": "Fluorine (2s²2p⁵) needs one electron to complete its shell.", "1": "The electron goes into the 3p orbital of chlorine (3s²3p⁵ → 3s²3p⁶).", "2": "Shielding and distance largely cancel the extra nuclear charge; the key factor is repulsion in the small F atom."}'),
  ('CHE-7031', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Chlorides of Period 3', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Which Period 3 chloride dissolves in water without hydrolysis, giving a neutral solution?', '["NaCl", "AlCl₃", "MgCl₂", "SiCl₄"]', 0, 'NaCl simply dissolves into hydrated ions (pH 7). Across the period, chlorides become more covalent and react with water: MgCl₂ gives a slightly acidic solution, AlCl₃ an acidic one, and SiCl₄ reacts vigorously to give HCl.', '{"1": "Hydrated Al³⁺ releases H⁺, so the solution is acidic.", "2": "Mg²⁺ is small and polarises water slightly, so the solution is weakly acidic.", "3": "SiCl₄ is hydrolysed: SiCl₄ + 2H₂O → SiO₂ + 4HCl."}'),
  ('CHE-7032', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Melting points across a period', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Which Period 3 element has the highest melting point, and why?', '["Aluminium, because it has the strongest metallic bonding in the period", "Sulphur, because S₈ molecules are larger than P₄ molecules", "Silicon, because it has a giant covalent structure with strong bonds throughout", "Phosphorus, because P₄ molecules are held by strong covalent bonds"]', 2, 'Melting Si means breaking many strong Si–Si covalent bonds in its giant lattice (about 1410 °C). Al is metallic (660 °C); P₄ and S₈ are simple molecules held by weak van der Waals forces.', '{"0": "Al''s metallic bonding is strong but Al melts at only 660 °C.", "1": "S₈ melts at about 115 °C because only weak intermolecular forces are overcome.", "3": "Melting P₄ breaks only weak forces between molecules, not covalent bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-7033', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Periodic properties', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'An element has the electronic configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p³. Which statement about it is correct?', '["Its atomic radius is larger than that of aluminium", "It forms a basic oxide", "It is a metal that conducts electricity", "Its first ionisation energy is higher than that of the element after it in the period"]', 3, 'This is phosphorus. Its half-filled 3p subshell has no paired electrons, so its first IE (1012 kJ mol⁻¹) is higher than that of sulphur (1000 kJ mol⁻¹).', '{"0": "Atomic radius decreases across the period, so P is smaller than Al.", "1": "Phosphorus oxides (P₄O₆, P₄O₁₀) are acidic.", "2": "Phosphorus is a non-metal and does not conduct."}'),
  ('CHE-7034', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Hydration of ions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Li⁺ has the most negative hydration enthalpy of the Group 1 ions. The best reason is that Li⁺', '["has the highest nuclear charge of the Group 1 ions", "is the smallest Group 1 ion, so its charge density is highest and it attracts water molecules most strongly", "forms covalent bonds with water molecules", "has the most electrons of the Group 1 ions"]', 1, 'All Group 1 ions have a +1 charge, but Li⁺ is by far the smallest, so the charge is concentrated and ion–dipole attractions to water are strongest. This is also why Li reacts with water less vigorously than its large hydration energy alone might suggest.', '{"0": "Li has the lowest nuclear charge (3) in the group.", "2": "Hydration is an ion–dipole attraction, not covalent bond formation.", "3": "Li⁺ has only two electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-7035', 'States of matter', 'Ideal gas equation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.7', 'A gas has a density of 1.25 g dm⁻³ at STP. Which gas could it be? (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16)', '["N₂", "O₂", "NH₃", "CO₂"]', 0, 'Molar mass = density × molar volume = 1.25 × 22.4 = 28 g mol⁻¹, which matches N₂ (CO would also fit).', '{"1": "O₂ (32) would have a density of 1.43 g dm⁻³.", "2": "NH₃ (17) would have a density of 0.76 g dm⁻³.", "3": "CO₂ (44) would have a density of 1.96 g dm⁻³."}'),
  ('CHE-7036', 'States of matter', 'Gas laws', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.4; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.5', '300 cm³ of a gas at 27 °C and 1.0 atm is heated to 127 °C and compressed to 2.0 atm. Its new volume is', '["150 cm³", "200 cm³", "706 cm³", "800 cm³"]', 1, 'P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂ with temperatures in kelvin: V₂ = 300 × (1.0/2.0) × (400/300) = 200 cm³.', '{"0": "This ignores the temperature change.", "2": "This uses Celsius temperatures (127/27) instead of kelvin.", "3": "This uses the pressure ratio the wrong way (×2 instead of ×½)."}'),
  ('CHE-7037', 'States of matter', 'Ideal gas equation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.7; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.8', '0.64 g of a gas occupies 0.492 dm³ at 27 °C and 1.0 atm. What is its molar mass? (R = 0.0821 dm³ atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)', '["2.9 g mol⁻¹", "32 g mol⁻¹", "64 g mol⁻¹", "3.2 × 10³ g mol⁻¹"]', 1, 'n = PV/RT = (1.0 × 0.492)/(0.0821 × 300) = 0.020 mol. M = 0.64/0.020 = 32 g mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses 27 instead of 300 K for T.", "2": "This would need only 0.010 mol of gas, half the amount present.", "3": "This uses R = 8.314, which does not match volume in dm³ and pressure in atm."}'),
  ('CHE-7038', 'States of matter', 'Boyle''s law', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.4', 'For a fixed mass of an ideal gas at constant temperature, which graph is a straight line passing through the origin?', '["V against temperature in °C", "P against V", "P against 1/V", "PV against P"]', 2, 'Boyle''s law: PV = constant, so P = k × (1/V). A plot of P against 1/V is a straight line through the origin with slope k.', '{"0": "V against °C is a straight line (Charles''s law, at constant pressure) but meets the axis at −273 °C, not the origin.", "1": "P against V is a curve (a hyperbola).", "3": "PV against P is a horizontal line, which does not pass through the origin."}'),
  ('CHE-7039', 'States of matter', 'Partial pressure', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.7', 'A vessel contains 4.0 g of hydrogen and 32 g of oxygen at a total pressure of 3.0 atm. The partial pressure of oxygen is', '["0.33 atm", "1.0 atm", "1.5 atm", "2.0 atm"]', 1, 'H₂ = 4.0/2 = 2.0 mol; O₂ = 32/32 = 1.0 mol. Mole fraction of O₂ = 1/3, so p(O₂) = ⅓ × 3.0 = 1.0 atm.', '{"0": "This is the mole fraction of O₂, not its partial pressure.", "2": "This splits the pressure equally, as if the moles were equal.", "3": "This is the partial pressure of hydrogen."}'),
  ('CHE-7040', 'States of matter', 'Real and ideal gases', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.9', 'At the same temperature and pressure, which gas deviates most from ideal behaviour?', '["H₂", "He", "N₂", "NH₃"]', 3, 'Deviation is largest for gases with strong intermolecular attractions. NH₃ is polar and forms hydrogen bonds, and it is easily liquefied, so its behaviour departs most from PV = nRT.', '{"0": "H₂ has very weak intermolecular forces.", "1": "He has tiny atoms and only very weak forces; it is the most nearly ideal.", "2": "N₂ is non-polar with weak forces."}'),
  ('CHE-7041', 'States of matter', 'Charles''s law and absolute zero', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.5; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.6', 'At constant pressure, to what temperature must a gas at 0 °C be heated to double its volume?', '["546 °C", "273 K", "273 °C", "136.5 °C"]', 2, 'V ∝ T in kelvin. 0 °C = 273 K, so doubling V needs T = 546 K, which is 546 − 273 = 273 °C.', '{"0": "546 is the answer in kelvin, not in °C.", "1": "273 K is 0 °C, the starting temperature.", "3": "Halving the kelvin temperature would halve the volume, not double it."}'),
  ('CHE-7042', 'States of matter', 'Partial pressure', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.4', 'A 2.0 dm³ bulb of N₂ at 3.0 atm is connected to a 3.0 dm³ bulb of O₂ at 2.0 atm, at constant temperature. When the gases have mixed, the total pressure is', '["1.2 atm", "2.4 atm", "2.5 atm", "5.0 atm"]', 1, 'Each gas expands into 5.0 dm³ (Boyle''s law): p(N₂) = 3.0 × 2.0/5.0 = 1.2 atm; p(O₂) = 2.0 × 3.0/5.0 = 1.2 atm. Total = 2.4 atm (Dalton''s law).', '{"0": "This is the partial pressure of only one gas.", "2": "This averages the two pressures without weighting by volume.", "3": "This adds the original pressures without allowing for expansion."}'),
  ('CHE-7043', 'States of matter', 'Kinetic molecular theory', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.1; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.2', 'At the same temperature, how does the root-mean-square speed of H₂ molecules compare with that of O₂ molecules? (H = 1, O = 16)', '["H₂ is 4 times faster", "H₂ is 16 times faster", "H₂ is 4 times slower", "They move at the same speed"]', 0, 'At the same temperature both gases have the same average kinetic energy, ½mu². So u ∝ 1/√M: u(H₂)/u(O₂) = √(32/2) = √16 = 4.', '{"1": "16 is the ratio of the molar masses; speed depends on the square root.", "2": "The lighter molecule moves faster.", "3": "They have the same average kinetic energy, not the same speed."}'),
  ('CHE-7044', 'States of matter', 'Vapour pressure and boiling point', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 4.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 4.2', 'At 25 °C, liquid X has a vapour pressure of 40 kPa and liquid Y a vapour pressure of 10 kPa. Which statement is correct?', '["X has stronger intermolecular forces and a higher boiling point than Y", "X evaporates more slowly than Y", "At its boiling point, X has a lower vapour pressure than Y has at its boiling point", "X has weaker intermolecular forces and a lower boiling point than Y"]', 3, 'Higher vapour pressure at the same temperature means molecules escape more easily, so the attractions are weaker; X reaches a vapour pressure equal to the external pressure at a lower temperature, so it boils lower.', '{"0": "Stronger forces would lower the vapour pressure.", "1": "Higher vapour pressure means faster evaporation.", "2": "At their boiling points both liquids have a vapour pressure equal to the external pressure."}'),
  ('CHE-7045', 'States of matter', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 4.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 4.3', 'Fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen, yet water (100 °C) boils at a much higher temperature than HF (about 20 °C). The best explanation is that', '["water has a higher molar mass than HF", "HF molecules are non-polar", "each H₂O molecule has two H atoms and two lone pairs, so it can form about twice as many hydrogen bonds as an HF molecule", "the O–H···O hydrogen bond is much stronger than the F–H···F hydrogen bond"]', 2, 'In HF each molecule has three lone pairs but only one H, so on average it forms one hydrogen bond (zig-zag chains). Water has two H and two lone pairs, giving a 3-D network with about two hydrogen bonds per molecule.', '{"0": "HF (20) has a slightly higher molar mass than H₂O (18).", "1": "HF is strongly polar.", "3": "The F–H···F hydrogen bond is actually stronger than O–H···O; the number of bonds is what matters."}'),
  ('CHE-7046', 'States of matter', 'Anomalous behaviour of water', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 4.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 4.4', 'When water is warmed from 0 °C to 4 °C, its volume', '["increases, because all liquids expand on heating", "decreases, because water molecules become smaller", "stays the same, because hydrogen bonds do not change below 4 °C", "decreases, because the open hydrogen-bonded structure collapses faster than thermal expansion increases the volume"]', 3, 'Near 0 °C water still holds ice-like open clusters. As it warms, these break and molecules pack more closely, which outweighs normal expansion until 4 °C, where the density is greatest. Above 4 °C water expands normally.', '{"0": "Water is an exception between 0 and 4 °C.", "1": "The molecules do not change size; their packing changes.", "2": "Hydrogen-bonded clusters break up progressively as water warms."}'),
  ('CHE-7047', 'States of matter', 'Covalent network crystals', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 5.1; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 5.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 5.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 5.3', 'Diamond and graphite are both giant covalent forms of carbon, but only graphite conducts electricity. The reason is that in graphite', '["each carbon is bonded to only three others (sp²), leaving one electron per atom delocalised over the layers", "carbon atoms lose electrons to become C⁴⁺ ions", "each carbon is bonded to four others by stronger bonds than in diamond", "the layers are held together by ionic bonds"]', 0, 'In graphite each C forms three σ bonds in a flat hexagonal layer; the fourth electron of each atom is delocalised across the layer and can move along it. In diamond all four electrons are used in localised σ bonds (sp³), so none are free to move.', '{"1": "Graphite contains no ions.", "2": "Four bonds per carbon is diamond''s structure, which leaves no free electrons.", "3": "The layers are held by weak van der Waals forces, not ionic bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-7048', 'States of matter', 'Ionic and molecular crystals', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 5.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 5.3', 'A solid is soft, sublimes on gentle warming and does not conduct electricity either as a solid or when melted. It is most likely', '["a covalent network crystal such as quartz", "an ionic crystal such as sodium chloride", "a metallic crystal such as copper", "a molecular crystal such as iodine"]', 3, 'Molecular crystals are held by weak intermolecular forces, so they are soft with low melting points, and they contain no ions or free electrons to carry current.', '{"0": "Network solids are very hard with very high melting points.", "1": "Ionic crystals are hard, melt high and conduct when molten.", "2": "Metals conduct in the solid state."}'),
  ('CHE-7049', 'States of matter', 'Lattice energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 5.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 5.5', 'Which compound has the most exothermic lattice energy?', '["MgO", "NaF", "KBr", "NaCl"]', 0, 'Lattice energy grows with ionic charge and with smaller ions. MgO has 2+ and 2− ions that are small, so its lattice energy (about −3800 kJ mol⁻¹) is far larger than those of the 1+/1− salts.', '{"1": "NaF has only singly charged ions (about −920 kJ mol⁻¹).", "2": "K⁺ and Br⁻ are the largest ions here, giving the smallest lattice energy.", "3": "Cl⁻ is larger than F⁻, so NaCl''s lattice energy is smaller than NaF''s."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7050', 'States of matter', 'Ideal gas equation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 3.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 3.7', 'What volume does 1.0 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 273 °C and 2.0 atm?', '["11.2 dm³", "22.4 dm³", "44.8 dm³", "89.6 dm³"]', 1, 'At STP (273 K, 1 atm) 1 mol occupies 22.4 dm³. 273 °C = 546 K doubles the volume, and 2.0 atm halves it, so V = 22.4 × 2 × ½ = 22.4 dm³.', '{"0": "This halves for pressure but forgets the temperature change.", "2": "This doubles for temperature but forgets to halve for pressure.", "3": "This applies both changes the wrong way."}'),
  ('CHE-7051', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.1; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.2', '2.0 mol of H₂ and 1.0 mol of I₂ are heated in a sealed 1.0 dm³ flask: H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI. At equilibrium 1.6 mol of HI is present. What is Kc?', '["0.094", "6.7", "10.7", "64"]', 2, 'Forming 1.6 mol HI uses 0.8 mol each of H₂ and I₂, leaving H₂ = 1.2 and I₂ = 0.2 mol dm⁻³. Kc = 1.6²/(1.2 × 0.2) = 2.56/0.24 = 10.7.', '{"0": "This is the inverse expression.", "1": "This uses [HI] instead of [HI]².", "3": "This wrongly takes 0.2 mol dm⁻³ for both H₂ and I₂, as if they started equal."}'),
  ('CHE-7052', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.2', 'What are the units of Kc for 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)?', '["dm³ mol⁻¹", "mol dm⁻³", "dm⁶ mol⁻²", "Kc has no units"]', 0, 'Kc = [SO₃]²/([SO₂]²[O₂]) = (mol dm⁻³)² / (mol dm⁻³)³ = (mol dm⁻³)⁻¹ = dm³ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This would be the unit if there were one more mole of product than reactant.", "2": "This would need Δn = −2.", "3": "Kc has no units only when the total moles of gas on each side are equal."}'),
  ('CHE-7053', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Reaction quotient', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.2', 'For N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), Kc = 0.36 mol dm⁻³ at a certain temperature. A mixture at this temperature has [N₂O₄] = 0.20 and [NO₂] = 0.20 mol dm⁻³. What happens?', '["More N₂O₄ forms, because Qc is greater than Kc", "More N₂O₄ forms, because the concentrations of the two gases are equal", "More N₂O₄ dissociates until equilibrium is reached, because Qc (0.20) is less than Kc", "Nothing; the mixture is already at equilibrium"]', 2, 'Qc = [NO₂]²/[N₂O₄] = 0.040/0.20 = 0.20 mol dm⁻³. Since Qc < Kc, the forward reaction runs until Qc rises to 0.36.', '{"0": "Qc is 0.20, which is smaller than Kc.", "1": "Equal concentrations do not mean equilibrium; Qc must equal Kc.", "3": "Qc ≠ Kc, so the mixture is not at equilibrium."}'),
  ('CHE-7054', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Solubility product', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.4', 'The solubility of CaF₂ in water at 25 °C is 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³. What is its Ksp?', '["8.0 × 10⁻¹²", "3.2 × 10⁻¹¹", "4.0 × 10⁻⁸", "1.6 × 10⁻⁷"]', 1, 'CaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2F⁻. If s = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴, then [Ca²⁺] = s and [F⁻] = 2s. Ksp = s(2s)² = 4s³ = 4 × 8.0 × 10⁻¹² = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹.', '{"0": "This is s³; the factor of 4 from (2s)² is missing.", "2": "This is s², the expression for a 1 : 1 salt.", "3": "This is 4s², which forgets one factor of s."}'),
  ('CHE-7055', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Common ion effect', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.5', 'Ksp of AgCl is 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ at 25 °C. What is its solubility in 0.10 mol dm⁻³ NaCl solution?', '["1.8 × 10⁻¹¹ mol dm⁻³", "1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol dm⁻³", "1.8 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³", "1.3 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³"]', 2, '[Cl⁻] ≈ 0.10 from the NaCl, so s = [Ag⁺] = Ksp/[Cl⁻] = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰/0.10 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³, about 7000 times less than in pure water.', '{"0": "This divides by 10 instead of 0.10.", "1": "This is Ksp itself, not a solubility.", "3": "This is the solubility in pure water (√Ksp); the common Cl⁻ ion lowers it."}'),
  ('CHE-7056', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Solubility product', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.4', 'Ksp of Ag₂CrO₄ is 1.1 × 10⁻¹² at 25 °C. What is the highest concentration of Ag⁺ that can exist in a solution containing 0.010 mol dm⁻³ CrO₄²⁻ before Ag₂CrO₄ starts to precipitate?', '["1.1 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol dm⁻³", "1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol dm⁻³", "5.2 × 10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³", "1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³"]', 3, 'Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻], so [Ag⁺] = √(Ksp/[CrO₄²⁻]) = √(1.1 × 10⁻¹²/0.010) = √(1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³.', '{"0": "This multiplies Ksp by [CrO₄²⁻] instead of dividing.", "1": "This forgets the square root ([Ag⁺] is squared in Ksp).", "2": "This halves the correct value, but [Ag⁺] itself is what is asked."}'),
  ('CHE-7057', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Le Chatelier''s principle', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.3', 'For N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), ΔH = +58 kJ mol⁻¹, which single change increases both the equilibrium yield of NO₂ and the rate of reaction?', '["Removing some NO₂", "Raising the pressure", "Adding a catalyst", "Raising the temperature"]', 3, 'The forward reaction is endothermic, so heating shifts the equilibrium to the right; heating also speeds up both reactions.', '{"0": "Removing NO₂ shifts the equilibrium right but lowers concentrations, so it does not speed up the reaction.", "1": "Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles (N₂O₄), lowering the yield of NO₂.", "2": "A catalyst speeds up reaching equilibrium but does not change the yield."}'),
  ('CHE-7058', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Le Chatelier''s principle', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.3', 'Argon is added at constant volume and temperature to an equilibrium mixture of N₂, H₂ and NH₃. What happens to the equilibrium?', '["It does not shift, because the partial pressures (concentrations) of N₂, H₂ and NH₃ are unchanged", "It shifts towards NH₃, because argon acts as a catalyst", "It shifts towards NH₃, because the total pressure has increased", "It shifts towards N₂ and H₂, because argon dilutes the ammonia"]', 0, 'At constant volume, an inert gas raises the total pressure but leaves each reacting gas''s partial pressure and concentration the same, so Qc still equals Kc and there is no shift.', '{"1": "Argon is inert and is not a catalyst.", "2": "Only the partial pressures of the reacting gases matter, and they have not changed.", "3": "The concentrations of all reacting gases are unchanged."}'),
  ('CHE-7059', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Haber process', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.7', 'For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹, the temperature is raised from 400 °C to 500 °C. Which statement is correct?', '["Kc decreases, but the rate of reaching equilibrium increases", "Kc is unchanged, because only a catalyst can change it", "Kc increases and the rate increases", "Kc decreases and the rate decreases"]', 0, 'For an exothermic reaction, raising the temperature favours the reverse reaction, so Kc gets smaller. All reactions go faster at higher temperature, which is why a compromise of about 400–450 °C is used.', '{"1": "Kc depends on temperature; a catalyst never changes Kc.", "2": "Kc falls with temperature for an exothermic reaction.", "3": "Rates increase with temperature."}'),
  ('CHE-7060', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'pH of strong acids', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', 'What is the pH of 0.0010 mol dm⁻³ sulphuric acid, assuming it is fully dissociated?', '["2.0", "2.7", "3.0", "3.3"]', 1, 'H₂SO₄ → 2H⁺ + SO₄²⁻, so [H⁺] = 2 × 0.0010 = 0.0020 mol dm⁻³. pH = −log(0.0020) = 2.7.', '{"0": "This would need [H⁺] = 0.010.", "2": "This counts only one H⁺ per H₂SO₄.", "3": "This halves [H⁺] instead of doubling it."}'),
  ('CHE-7061', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'pH of mixtures', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', '50 cm³ of 0.20 mol dm⁻³ HCl is mixed with 50 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. What is the pH of the mixture?', '["0.7", "1.0", "1.3", "7.0"]', 2, 'H⁺ = 0.010 mol; OH⁻ = 0.0050 mol. Excess H⁺ = 0.0050 mol in 100 cm³, so [H⁺] = 0.050 mol dm⁻³ and pH = −log 0.050 = 1.3.', '{"0": "This is the pH of the original 0.20 mol dm⁻³ acid.", "1": "This uses the excess in 50 cm³ instead of the total 100 cm³.", "3": "The acid is in excess, so the mixture is not neutral."}'),
  ('CHE-7062', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'pH of weak acids', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', 'What is the pH of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid? (Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³)', '["1.0", "2.9", "4.7", "5.7"]', 1, '[H⁺] = √(Ka × c) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.34 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³, so pH = 2.87 ≈ 2.9.', '{"0": "This treats the weak acid as fully dissociated.", "2": "This is the pKa, which equals the pH only in a half-neutralised buffer.", "3": "This is −log(Ka × c), forgetting the square root."}'),
  ('CHE-7063', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Buffer solutions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', '0.20 mol of ethanoic acid (pKa = 4.74) and 0.10 mol of NaOH are dissolved together in water to make 1.0 dm³. What is the pH?', '["2.87", "4.74", "7.00", "9.26"]', 1, 'NaOH converts 0.10 mol of the acid into 0.10 mol ethanoate, leaving 0.10 mol acid. Equal amounts of acid and salt make a buffer with pH = pKa + log(1) = 4.74.', '{"0": "This is about the pH of ethanoic acid alone.", "2": "Not all the acid is neutralised, so the solution is not neutral.", "3": "This is 14 − 4.74; the solution is an acidic buffer."}'),
  ('CHE-7064', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Buffer solutions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', 'A buffer contains 0.10 mol ethanoic acid and 0.10 mol sodium ethanoate in 1.0 dm³ (pKa = 4.74). 0.020 mol of HCl is added. The new pH is about', '["1.70", "4.56", "4.74", "4.92"]', 1, 'H⁺ reacts with ethanoate: acid becomes 0.12 mol and salt 0.08 mol. pH = 4.74 + log(0.08/0.12) = 4.74 − 0.18 = 4.56. The pH changes only slightly.', '{"0": "This is the pH of 0.020 mol dm⁻³ HCl in water with no buffer.", "2": "The ratio of salt to acid has changed, so the pH changes a little.", "3": "This uses log(acid/salt) instead of log(salt/acid)."}'),
  ('CHE-7065', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ionic product of water', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', 'At 50 °C, Kw = 5.5 × 10⁻¹⁴ and pure water has a pH of about 6.6. This water is', '["basic, because Kw has increased", "acidic, because its pH is below 7", "acidic, because heating releases extra H⁺ only", "neutral, because [H⁺] still equals [OH⁻]"]', 3, 'Ionisation of water is endothermic, so Kw increases with temperature and [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = √Kw = 2.3 × 10⁻⁷, giving pH 6.6. Neutral means [H⁺] = [OH⁻], not pH 7.', '{"0": "[OH⁻] rises by exactly as much as [H⁺].", "1": "pH 7 is neutral only at 25 °C.", "2": "Each water molecule that ionises gives one H⁺ and one OH⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-7066', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Salt hydrolysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.6', '0.10 mol dm⁻³ solutions of four salts are made. Which has the highest pH?', '["Na₂CO₃", "CH₃COONa", "NaCl", "NH₄Cl"]', 0, 'CO₃²⁻ is the conjugate base of the very weak acid HCO₃⁻ (Ka about 5 × 10⁻¹¹), so it is a much stronger base than CH₃COO⁻ (conjugate base of ethanoic acid, Ka 1.8 × 10⁻⁵).', '{"1": "Ethanoate is basic, but a much weaker base than carbonate.", "2": "NaCl does not hydrolyse; its solution is neutral.", "3": "NH₄⁺ hydrolyses to give an acidic solution."}'),
  ('CHE-7067', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.1; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.2', '2.0 mol of PCl₅ is heated in a 1.0 dm³ vessel: PCl₅ ⇌ PCl₃ + Cl₂. At equilibrium 40% of the PCl₅ has dissociated. What is Kc?', '["0.27 mol dm⁻³", "0.53 mol dm⁻³", "0.64 mol dm⁻³", "1.9 mol dm⁻³"]', 1, '40% of 2.0 mol = 0.80 mol dissociates: [PCl₅] = 1.2, [PCl₃] = [Cl₂] = 0.80 mol dm⁻³. Kc = (0.80 × 0.80)/1.2 = 0.53 mol dm⁻³.', '{"0": "This uses 0.40 and 0.60 as if the start were 1.0 mol.", "2": "This forgets to divide by [PCl₅].", "3": "This is the inverse expression, [PCl₅]/([PCl₃][Cl₂])."}'),
  ('CHE-7068', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 6.2', 'For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ the equilibrium constant is K. What is the equilibrium constant, at the same temperature, for NH₃ ⇌ ½N₂ + 3⁄2H₂?', '["−K/2", "√K", "1/K", "1/√K"]', 3, 'Reversing an equation inverts K (1/K); halving the coefficients takes the square root. So K′ = (1/K)^½ = 1/√K.', '{"0": "Equilibrium constants are never negative; changes multiply or take powers of K.", "1": "This halves the equation but does not reverse it.", "2": "This reverses the equation but does not halve it."}'),
  ('CHE-7069', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate law from data', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.2; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.4', 'For A + B → products: Exp 1, [A] = 0.10, [B] = 0.10, rate = 2.0 × 10⁻³; Exp 2, [A] = 0.20, [B] = 0.10, rate = 4.0 × 10⁻³; Exp 3, [A] = 0.10, [B] = 0.20, rate = 8.0 × 10⁻³ (mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹). The rate equation is', '["rate = k[A]²[B]", "rate = k[A]²[B]²", "rate = k[A][B]²", "rate = k[A][B]"]', 2, 'Doubling [A] (Exp 1 → 2) doubles the rate: first order in A. Doubling [B] (Exp 1 → 3) quadruples the rate: second order in B. So rate = k[A][B]², third order overall.', '{"0": "This swaps the orders of A and B.", "1": "Doubling [A] only doubles the rate, so A is first order.", "3": "Doubling [B] gives four times the rate, so B is second order."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7070', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate constant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.8', 'A reaction is third order overall. With concentrations in mol dm⁻³ and time in seconds, the units of its rate constant are', '["dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹", "mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "s⁻¹", "dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹"]', 0, 'k = rate/[conc]³ = (mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹)/(mol dm⁻³)³ = mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹.', '{"1": "These are the units for a zero-order reaction.", "2": "These are the units for a first-order reaction.", "3": "These are the units for a second-order reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-7071', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Half-life', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2', 'A first-order reactant has a half-life of 20 minutes. Starting with 80 g, how much remains after 1 hour?', '["5 g", "10 g", "20 g", "26.7 g"]', 1, '1 hour = 3 half-lives: 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 g.', '{"0": "This is after four half-lives (80 minutes).", "2": "This is after 40 minutes (two half-lives).", "3": "This divides 80 by 3; decay is not linear."}'),
  ('CHE-7072', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Half-life', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2', 'A first-order reaction has a half-life of 12 minutes. How long does it take for 93.75% of the reactant to be used up?', '["22.5 minutes", "36 minutes", "48 minutes", "60 minutes"]', 2, '6.25% remains, which is 1/16 = (½)⁴, so it takes 4 half-lives: 4 × 12 = 48 minutes.', '{"0": "This scales the half-life linearly (93.75/50 × 12).", "1": "After 36 minutes (3 half-lives) 87.5% has reacted.", "3": "After 60 minutes (5 half-lives) about 96.9% has reacted."}'),
  ('CHE-7073', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Order of reaction', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2', 'A graph of reactant concentration against time is a straight line sloping downwards. Which statement is correct?', '["The rate decreases steadily as the reactant is used up", "The reaction is second order", "The reaction is first order with a constant half-life", "The reaction is zero order and its rate does not depend on the concentration"]', 3, 'A constant slope means a constant rate, so the rate does not change as the concentration falls: zero order (rate = k). For example, some reactions on a saturated catalyst surface.', '{"0": "A straight line means the rate is constant, not falling.", "1": "A second-order reaction gives a curve that flattens even faster.", "2": "A first-order reaction gives a curve with a constant half-life, not a straight line."}'),
  ('CHE-7074', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate equation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.4; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.7', 'For 2NO(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO₂(g), rate = k[NO]²[O₂]. If the volume of the vessel is suddenly halved at constant temperature, the rate becomes', '["2 times as great", "4 times as great", "8 times as great", "16 times as great"]', 2, 'Halving the volume doubles every concentration. Rate = k(2[NO])²(2[O₂]) = 8 × the original rate.', '{"0": "This doubles only [O₂].", "1": "This doubles only [NO].", "3": "This raises the factor 2 to the fourth power instead of the third."}'),
  ('CHE-7075', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Temperature and rate', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.7', 'The rate of a reaction doubles for every 10 °C rise in temperature. Starting at 25 °C, at what temperature will the reaction go 32 times as fast?', '["50 °C", "57 °C", "75 °C", "345 °C"]', 2, '32 = 2⁵, so five 10 °C steps are needed: a rise of 50 °C, from 25 °C to 75 °C.', '{"0": "50 °C is the rise in temperature, not the final temperature.", "1": "This adds 32 to 25.", "3": "This adds 32 × 10 °C, treating the increase as linear."}'),
  ('CHE-7076', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Reaction mechanism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.2', 'NO₂ + CO → NO + CO₂ follows a two-step mechanism: Step 1 (slow) 2NO₂ → NO₃ + NO; Step 2 (fast) NO₃ + CO → NO₂ + CO₂. Which statement is correct?', '["The rate equation is rate = k[NO₂]², and NO₃ is an intermediate", "CO is an intermediate, so it does not appear in the rate equation", "The rate equation is rate = k[NO₃][CO], because step 2 forms the products", "The rate equation is rate = k[NO₂][CO], because these are the reactants"]', 0, 'The slow step sets the rate, so rate = k[NO₂]². NO₃ is made in step 1 and used in step 2, so it is an intermediate. CO reacts only after the slow step and does not appear.', '{"1": "CO is a reactant, not an intermediate.", "2": "Step 2 is fast and does not control the rate.", "3": "The rate equation comes from the slow step, not the overall equation."}'),
  ('CHE-7077', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate of reaction', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2', 'For 2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂, NO₂ is formed at 0.040 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. At what rate is N₂O₅ used up?', '["0.010 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "0.020 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "0.040 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "0.080 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹"]', 1, 'From the equation, 2 mol N₂O₅ give 4 mol NO₂, so N₂O₅ is used at half the rate NO₂ is formed: 0.040/2 = 0.020 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. (O₂ forms at 0.010.)', '{"0": "This is the rate of formation of O₂.", "2": "This ignores the mole ratio.", "3": "This uses the ratio the wrong way round."}'),
  ('CHE-7078', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.5', 'A reaction has Ea(forward) = 80 kJ mol⁻¹ and Ea(reverse) = 120 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst lowers Ea(forward) to 50 kJ mol⁻¹. Which is now correct?', '["Ea(reverse) = 90 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔH = −10 kJ mol⁻¹", "Ea(reverse) = 150 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔH = −40 kJ mol⁻¹", "Ea(reverse) = 120 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔH = −70 kJ mol⁻¹", "Ea(reverse) = 90 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔH = −40 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 3, 'ΔH = Ea(f) − Ea(r) = 80 − 120 = −40 kJ mol⁻¹, and a catalyst does not change ΔH. It lowers the peak, so both barriers drop by the same 30 kJ: Ea(reverse) = 90 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "ΔH is fixed by the reactant and product energies.", "1": "The reverse barrier falls, not rises.", "2": "A catalyst lowers both barriers, and ΔH cannot change."}'),
  ('CHE-7079', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Factors affecting rate', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.7', 'Two experiments use excess dilute HCl: one with 5 g of marble chips, the other with 5 g of powdered marble. Which statement about the volume of CO₂ against time is correct?', '["The powder gives a steeper initial slope, but both give the same final volume of CO₂", "Both give the same initial slope, because the mass of marble is the same", "The chips give a steeper initial slope because there are fewer particles", "The powder gives a larger final volume of CO₂"]', 0, 'Powder has a much larger surface area, so collisions with acid are more frequent and the reaction is faster. The amount of CaCO₃ (and acid is in excess) fixes the final volume of CO₂, which is the same.', '{"1": "Rate depends on surface area, not just on mass.", "2": "The chips react more slowly because they expose less surface.", "3": "The same mass of CaCO₃ gives the same amount of CO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-7080', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Order of reaction', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 7.2; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 7.4', 'When the initial concentration of a reactant is halved, the half-life of the reaction doubles. The reaction is', '["zero order", "third order", "first order", "second order"]', 3, 'For a second-order reaction t½ = 1/(k[A]₀), so the half-life is inversely proportional to the starting concentration: halving [A]₀ doubles t½.', '{"0": "For zero order, t½ = [A]₀/2k, so halving [A]₀ halves t½.", "1": "For third order, halving [A]₀ would make t½ four times longer.", "2": "For first order, t½ = 0.693/k, independent of concentration."}'),
  ('CHE-7081', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.6', 'Standard enthalpies of combustion (kJ mol⁻¹): C(graphite) −394, H₂(g) −286, C₂H₅OH(l) −1367. What is the standard enthalpy of formation of ethanol, 2C + 3H₂ + ½O₂ → C₂H₅OH?', '["−3013 kJ mol⁻¹", "−1367 kJ mol⁻¹", "−279 kJ mol⁻¹", "+279 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'ΔHf = Σ ΔHc(reactants) − ΔHc(product) = [2(−394) + 3(−286)] − (−1367) = −1646 + 1367 = −279 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This adds the ethanol term instead of subtracting it.", "1": "This is the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol, not its formation.", "3": "The sign is reversed: subtract the product''s combustion from the reactants'', not the other way."}'),
  ('CHE-7082', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energies', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.8; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.6', 'For ½H₂(g) + ½Br₂(g) → HBr(g), ΔH = −52 kJ mol⁻¹. The bond energies of H–H and Br–Br are 436 and 193 kJ mol⁻¹. What is the H–Br bond energy?', '["263 kJ mol⁻¹", "315 kJ mol⁻¹", "366 kJ mol⁻¹", "681 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'ΔH = bonds broken − bonds made: −52 = ½(436) + ½(193) − E(H–Br) = 314.5 − E. So E(H–Br) = 314.5 + 52 = 366 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This subtracts 52 instead of adding it (a sign error).", "1": "This is only the energy to break half a mole each of H₂ and Br₂.", "3": "This uses whole moles of H₂ and Br₂ instead of half moles."}'),
  ('CHE-7083', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energies', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.8; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.7', 'Using bond energies (kJ mol⁻¹) C–H 413, Cl–Cl 243, C–Cl 346, H–Cl 432, estimate ΔH for CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl.', '["−778 kJ mol⁻¹", "−365 kJ mol⁻¹", "−122 kJ mol⁻¹", "+122 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'Broken: one C–H and one Cl–Cl = 413 + 243 = 656. Formed: one C–Cl and one H–Cl = 346 + 432 = 778. ΔH = 656 − 778 = −122 kJ mol⁻¹. The other three C–H bonds are unchanged.', '{"0": "This counts only the bonds formed.", "1": "This leaves out the Cl–Cl bond that is broken.", "3": "The sign is reversed."}'),
  ('CHE-7084', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.7', 'Burning 1.60 g of methanol (M = 32) heats 200 g of water from 20.0 °C to 50.0 °C (c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹). What experimental enthalpy of combustion does this give?', '["−1003 kJ mol⁻¹", "−502 kJ mol⁻¹", "−251 kJ mol⁻¹", "−25.1 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 1, 'q = 200 × 4.18 × 30.0 = 25 080 J. Methanol = 1.60/32 = 0.0500 mol. ΔHc = −25.08 kJ/0.0500 mol = −502 kJ mol⁻¹. (The data-book value, about −726, is larger because heat is lost to the air and the apparatus.)', '{"0": "This uses 0.025 mol of methanol.", "2": "This uses 0.100 mol of methanol.", "3": "This is the heat released in kJ, not per mole."}'),
  ('CHE-7085', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.7', '50 cm³ of 2.0 mol dm⁻³ HCl is mixed with 50 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. ΔH(neutralisation) = −57 kJ mol⁻¹, c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹ and the mixture has a mass of 100 g. The temperature rise is about', '["3.4 °C", "6.8 °C", "10.2 °C", "13.6 °C"]', 1, 'Only 0.050 mol NaOH is present, so only 0.050 mol water forms (HCl is in excess). Heat = 0.050 × 57 000 = 2850 J. ΔT = 2850/(100 × 4.18) = 6.8 °C.', '{"0": "This halves the correct amount of heat.", "2": "This uses 0.075 mol, the average of the two amounts.", "3": "This uses 0.10 mol (the HCl), but the NaOH limits the reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-7086', 'Thermochemistry', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.5', 'For CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) at 298 K, how are ΔH and ΔE related? (ΔH = ΔE + ΔnRT, R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)', '["ΔH is about 5 kJ mol⁻¹ more negative than ΔE", "ΔH equals ΔE, because the moles of reactants and products are equal", "ΔH is about 5 kJ mol⁻¹ less negative than ΔE", "ΔH is about 2.5 kJ mol⁻¹ more negative than ΔE"]', 0, 'Gas moles: 1 (CO₂) − 3 (CH₄ + 2O₂) = −2, because the water is liquid. ΔnRT = −2 × 8.314 × 298 = −4.96 kJ, so ΔH = ΔE − 4.96 kJ: ΔH is more negative.', '{"1": "Only gas moles count, and they fall from 3 to 1.", "2": "Δn is negative, which makes ΔH more negative, not less.", "3": "This uses Δn = −1; liquid water does not count as gas."}'),
  ('CHE-7087', 'Thermochemistry', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.5', 'A reaction is carried out in a sealed, rigid bomb calorimeter, so its volume cannot change. The heat it releases is equal to', '["−(ΔH + ΔE)", "the work done by the gases", "−ΔH, because heat measured at any condition equals ΔH", "−ΔE, the change in internal energy, because no expansion work is done"]', 3, 'ΔE = q + w. At constant volume no pressure–volume work is done (w = 0), so q(v) = ΔE. At constant pressure the heat equals ΔH instead (q(p) = ΔH).', '{"0": "There is no such combination; q(v) = ΔE.", "1": "No expansion work is possible at constant volume.", "2": "Heat equals ΔH only at constant pressure."}'),
  ('CHE-7088', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.7', 'For which equation is the standard enthalpy change both the standard enthalpy of formation of the product and the standard enthalpy of combustion of a reactant?', '["C(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g)", "C(diamond) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g)", "CO(g) + ½O₂(g) → CO₂(g)", "2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l)"]', 0, 'Formation needs one mole of product from elements in their standard states; combustion needs one mole of a substance burnt completely. Burning 1 mol of graphite (carbon''s standard state) to 1 mol CO₂ fits both.', '{"1": "Diamond is not the standard state of carbon, so this is not the formation of CO₂.", "2": "CO is a compound, so this is not a formation reaction (it is the combustion of CO).", "3": "This forms 2 mol of water and burns 2 mol of H₂; both definitions are per mole."}'),
  ('CHE-7089', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.6', 'Given 2Fe(s) + 3⁄2O₂(g) → Fe₂O₃(s), ΔH = −824 kJ, and 2Al(s) + 3⁄2O₂(g) → Al₂O₃(s), ΔH = −1676 kJ, what is ΔH for the thermite reaction 2Al(s) + Fe₂O₃(s) → Al₂O₃(s) + 2Fe(s)?', '["−2500 kJ", "−852 kJ", "−426 kJ", "+852 kJ"]', 1, 'Add the Al equation to the reverse of the Fe equation: ΔH = −1676 + 824 = −852 kJ.', '{"0": "This adds both equations without reversing the iron one.", "2": "This halves the answer.", "3": "The sign is reversed."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7090', 'Thermochemistry', 'Exothermic and endothermic reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.2', 'Solid ammonium nitrate dissolves in water and the beaker becomes cold. Which statement is correct?', '["ΔH is positive, because the system releases heat to the surroundings", "ΔH is negative, because the temperature of the solution falls", "No energy change occurs; the cooling is due to evaporation", "ΔH is positive, and the energy absorbed by the system comes from the water and the beaker"]', 3, 'The process is endothermic: the system (dissolving salt) takes in heat from its surroundings (the water and beaker), which therefore cool down. ΔH > 0.', '{"0": "An endothermic system absorbs heat; it does not release it.", "1": "A fall in temperature of the surroundings means heat flowed into the system, so ΔH is positive.", "2": "The temperature drop is caused by the dissolving itself."}'),
  ('CHE-7091', 'Thermochemistry', 'State functions', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.3', 'A system changes from state 1 to state 2 by two different routes. Which quantities must have the same value for both routes?', '["q and w only", "ΔH, ΔE, q and w", "q only", "ΔH and ΔE only"]', 3, 'ΔH and ΔE are state functions: they depend only on the initial and final states. Heat (q) and work (w) depend on the path, though their sum, q + w = ΔE, does not.', '{"0": "Heat and work are path functions.", "1": "Only the state functions must be equal.", "2": "Heat depends on the route taken."}'),
  ('CHE-7092', 'Thermochemistry', 'Enthalpy of neutralisation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 8.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 8.7', 'The enthalpy of neutralisation of HCl by NaOH is −57.3 kJ mol⁻¹, but for ethanoic acid with NaOH it is about −55.2 kJ mol⁻¹. The difference arises because', '["some energy is used to complete the ionisation of the weak ethanoic acid", "ethanoic acid is a stronger acid than HCl", "ethanoic acid forms more water per mole than HCl", "sodium ethanoate is less soluble than sodium chloride"]', 0, 'For strong acids the reaction is only H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Ethanoic acid is mostly un-ionised, and ionising it is slightly endothermic, so less heat is released overall.', '{"1": "Ethanoic acid is a weak acid.", "2": "Both produce one mole of water per mole of acid.", "3": "Both salts stay dissolved; solubility is not the cause."}'),
  ('CHE-7093', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell potential', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.5', 'E°(Mg²⁺/Mg) = −2.37 V and E°(Ag⁺/Ag) = +0.80 V. What is the standard e.m.f. of the cell Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag?', '["1.57 V", "2.37 V", "3.17 V", "3.97 V"]', 2, 'E°cell = E°(cathode) − E°(anode) = 0.80 − (−2.37) = 3.17 V. Electrode potentials are not multiplied when an equation is multiplied.', '{"0": "This adds the two values with their signs.", "1": "This is only the magnesium value.", "3": "This doubles the silver potential because of the 2 in the equation, which is wrong."}'),
  ('CHE-7094', 'Electrochemistry', 'Feasibility of redox reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.5', 'E° values (V): Zn²⁺/Zn −0.76, Fe²⁺/Fe −0.44, Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34, Ag⁺/Ag +0.80. Which reaction will NOT occur under standard conditions?', '["Cu + Fe²⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Fe", "Zn + Fe²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Fe", "Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag", "Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu"]', 0, 'A metal reduces the ions of a metal with a more positive E°. For Cu + Fe²⁺, E°cell = −0.44 − 0.34 = −0.78 V, which is negative, so it does not occur.', '{"1": "E°cell = −0.44 − (−0.76) = +0.32 V: feasible.", "2": "E°cell = 0.80 − 0.34 = +0.46 V: feasible.", "3": "E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.78 V: feasible."}'),
  ('CHE-7095', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.2', 'In which species does sulphur have an (average) oxidation number of +2?', '["H₂S", "SO₃²⁻", "S₄O₆²⁻", "S₂O₃²⁻"]', 3, 'In S₂O₃²⁻: 2x + 3(−2) = −2, so x = +2. In S₄O₆²⁻: 4x − 12 = −2, x = +2.5. In SO₃²⁻: x = +4. In H₂S: x = −2.', '{"0": "Sulphur is −2.", "1": "Sulphur is +4.", "2": "The average is +2.5."}'),
  ('CHE-7096', 'Electrochemistry', 'Balancing redox equations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.6; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.3', 'In acidic solution, dichromate oxidises iron(II): Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + ?Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + ?Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O. How many Fe²⁺ ions react with one Cr₂O₇²⁻ ion?', '["2", "3", "5", "6"]', 3, 'Each Cr goes from +6 to +3, gaining 3 electrons; two Cr atoms gain 6. Each Fe²⁺ loses one electron, so 6 Fe²⁺ are needed.', '{"0": "This simply matches the two Cr atoms.", "1": "This counts electrons for only one Cr atom.", "2": "This is the ratio for MnO₄⁻, which gains 5 electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-7097', 'Electrochemistry', 'Redox titration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.6; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.3', '25.0 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ FeSO₄ is titrated with 0.020 mol dm⁻³ KMnO₄ in acid (MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O). What volume of KMnO₄ is needed?', '["5.0 cm³", "12.5 cm³", "25.0 cm³", "125 cm³"]', 2, 'Fe²⁺ = 0.0250 × 0.10 = 2.5 × 10⁻³ mol. MnO₄⁻ = 2.5 × 10⁻³/5 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol. V = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴/0.020 = 0.025 dm³ = 25.0 cm³.', '{"0": "This divides by 5 twice.", "1": "This uses a 1 : 10 ratio.", "3": "This multiplies by 5 instead of dividing."}'),
  ('CHE-7098', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.4', 'For how long must a current of 9.65 A flow through molten aluminium oxide (in cryolite) to deposit 0.90 g of aluminium? (Al = 27, F = 96 500 C mol⁻¹)', '["100 s", "333 s", "1000 s", "3000 s"]', 2, 'Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al. Al = 0.90/27 = 0.0333 mol, needing 3 × 0.0333 = 0.100 mol of electrons = 0.100 × 96 500 = 9650 C. t = Q/I = 9650/9.65 = 1000 s.', '{"0": "This is ten times too short.", "1": "This forgets that each Al³⁺ needs three electrons.", "3": "This multiplies by 3 twice."}'),
  ('CHE-7099', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.4; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.4', 'Dilute sulphuric acid is electrolysed with platinum electrodes, and 30 cm³ of gas is collected at the cathode. What volume of gas is collected at the anode under the same conditions?', '["15 cm³ of oxygen", "30 cm³ of oxygen", "15 cm³ of sulphur dioxide", "60 cm³ of oxygen"]', 0, 'Cathode: 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂. Anode: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. The same charge gives 2 mol H₂ for every 1 mol O₂, so 30 cm³ H₂ goes with 15 cm³ O₂.', '{"1": "Equal volumes would need equal electrons per molecule, but O₂ needs 4 and H₂ only 2.", "2": "Sulphate ions are not discharged; water is oxidised to oxygen.", "3": "This reverses the 2 : 1 ratio."}'),
  ('CHE-7100', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard hydrogen electrode', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.4', 'The standard hydrogen electrode is given a potential of exactly 0.00 V. Which change would make its potential no longer 0.00 V?', '["Using 0.10 mol dm⁻³ H⁺ instead of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ H⁺", "Using a larger piece of platinised platinum", "Bubbling the hydrogen faster at the same pressure", "Connecting it to a different half-cell"]', 0, 'Standard conditions are 1 mol dm⁻³ H⁺, H₂ at 1 atm (101 kPa) and 298 K. Changing [H⁺] changes the position of the 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ H₂ equilibrium and so the potential.', '{"1": "Electrode size does not affect the potential.", "2": "The pressure, not the bubbling rate, matters.", "3": "The SHE''s own potential is fixed; the cell e.m.f. depends on the other half-cell."}'),
  ('CHE-7101', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode potentials', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.5', 'E° values (V): Cl₂/Cl⁻ +1.36, MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺ (acid) +1.51, Cr₂O₇²⁻/Cr³⁺ (acid) +1.33, Br₂/Br⁻ +1.07. Under standard conditions, which can oxidise Cl⁻ to Cl₂?', '["Acidified Cr₂O₇²⁻", "Acidified MnO₄⁻", "Br₂", "None of them"]', 1, 'Only an oxidant with E° more positive than +1.36 V can oxidise Cl⁻. MnO₄⁻ (+1.51 V) can; this is why KMnO₄ titrations are acidified with H₂SO₄, not HCl.', '{"0": "+1.33 V is slightly below +1.36 V.", "2": "+1.07 V is well below +1.36 V.", "3": "MnO₄⁻ has a high enough E°."}'),
  ('CHE-7102', 'Electrochemistry', 'Redox reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.1', 'Which reaction is a disproportionation?', '["2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂", "Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu", "2H₂S + SO₂ → 3S + 2H₂O", "3Cl₂ + 6OH⁻ → 5Cl⁻ + ClO₃⁻ + 3H₂O"]', 3, 'In disproportionation one element in a single oxidation state is both oxidised and reduced. Chlorine (0) becomes Cl⁻ (−1) and ClO₃⁻ (+5).', '{"0": "Cl is reduced (+5 → −1) but oxygen is oxidised (−2 → 0): two different elements.", "1": "Two different elements are oxidised and reduced: a displacement reaction.", "2": "Sulphur in two different states (−2 and +4) meets in the middle (0): the reverse of disproportionation."}'),
  ('CHE-7103', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.3', 'In 2KMnO₄ + 16HCl → 2KCl + 2MnCl₂ + 5Cl₂ + 8H₂O, what fraction of the HCl acts as a reducing agent?', '["1", "1/2", "5/8", "3/8"]', 2, 'Only the Cl atoms that become Cl₂ are oxidised: 5Cl₂ contains 10 Cl, so 10 of the 16 HCl are reducing agents (10/16 = 5/8). The other 6 Cl end up as Cl⁻ in KCl and MnCl₂.', '{"0": "Some chlorine stays as Cl⁻ and is not oxidised.", "1": "10 of 16 is more than half.", "3": "This is the fraction that only provides Cl⁻ ions."}'),
  ('CHE-7104', 'Electrochemistry', 'Galvanic cells', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.4', 'In a Daniell cell (Zn | Zn²⁺ || Cu²⁺ | Cu) with a KNO₃ salt bridge, the nitrate ions in the salt bridge move towards', '["the zinc half-cell, to balance the Zn²⁺ ions being formed there", "the copper half-cell, to replace the Cu²⁺ ions being removed", "the copper half-cell, because electrons arrive there", "neither half-cell; the salt bridge ions do not move"]', 0, 'At the zinc anode, Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ adds positive ions to the solution, so anions (NO₃⁻) move there to keep it neutral; K⁺ ions move to the copper half-cell, where Cu²⁺ is removed.', '{"1": "Removing Cu²⁺ leaves excess negative charge, so cations (K⁺) move there, not anions.", "2": "Electrons travel through the wire, not the bridge; anions move towards the anode compartment.", "3": "The ions must move to keep both solutions neutral, or the current stops."}'),
  ('CHE-7105', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR theory', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.1; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.4', 'Which species has bond angles of exactly 109.5°?', '["H₃O⁺", "NH₃", "H₂O", "NH₄⁺"]', 3, 'NH₄⁺ has four bonding pairs and no lone pair around N, so the four N–H bonds point to the corners of a regular tetrahedron (109.5°). Each lone pair in the other species squeezes the bond angles below 109.5° (NH₃ 107°, H₃O⁺ about 107°, H₂O 104.5°).', '{"0": "One lone pair reduces the angle to about 107°.", "1": "One lone pair reduces the angle to about 107°.", "2": "Two lone pairs reduce the angle to 104.5°."}'),
  ('CHE-7106', 'Chemical bonding', 'Molecular polarity', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.5', 'Which pair of molecules are both non-polar even though they contain polar bonds?', '["H₂O and NH₃", "CO₂ and SO₂", "CCl₄ and CHCl₃", "CO₂ and BCl₃"]', 3, 'CO₂ is linear and BCl₃ is trigonal planar, so their bond dipoles cancel exactly.', '{"0": "Both are polar: bent and pyramidal shapes do not cancel the dipoles.", "1": "SO₂ is bent (it has a lone pair), so it is polar.", "2": "CHCl₃ is polar because the C–H and C–Cl dipoles do not cancel."}'),
  ('CHE-7107', 'Chemical bonding', 'Hybridisation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.3', 'In propadiene, CH₂=C=CH₂, the hybridisation of the middle carbon atom is', '["sp³", "sp", "sp²", "dsp²"]', 1, 'The central carbon forms two σ bonds (one to each CH₂) and two π bonds, so it uses two hybrid orbitals: sp, with a linear C=C=C arrangement. The end carbons are sp².', '{"0": "An sp³ carbon forms four σ bonds and no π bonds.", "2": "The end carbons are sp², not the middle one.", "3": "Carbon does not use d orbitals."}'),
  ('CHE-7108', 'Chemical bonding', 'Sigma and pi bonds', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.2', 'How many σ and π bonds are there in propenenitrile, CH₂=CH–C≡N?', '["7 σ and 2 π", "6 σ and 2 π", "6 σ and 3 π", "5 σ and 4 π"]', 2, 'σ: three C–H, C=C (1), C–C (1), C≡N (1) = 6. π: one in C=C and two in C≡N = 3.', '{"0": "This misses one π bond and adds an extra σ.", "1": "A triple bond contains two π bonds.", "3": "This counts the C≡N as having two σ bonds; a triple bond has one σ and two π."}'),
  ('CHE-7109', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR theory', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.4', 'Which species has the same shape as the ammonia molecule?', '["H₃O⁺", "CH₃⁺", "BF₃", "SO₃"]', 0, 'NH₃ and H₃O⁺ both have three bonding pairs and one lone pair around the central atom, giving a trigonal pyramidal shape.', '{"1": "CH₃⁺ has three bonding pairs and no lone pair: trigonal planar.", "2": "BF₃ has no lone pair: trigonal planar.", "3": "SO₃ is trigonal planar."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7110', 'Chemical bonding', 'Ionic character of covalent bonds', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.6', 'Which compound has the greatest covalent character?', '["NaCl", "LiI", "LiF", "CsF"]', 1, 'Covalent character grows when a small, highly charged cation polarises a large, easily distorted anion. Li⁺ is the smallest cation and I⁻ the largest anion here.', '{"0": "Na⁺ is larger than Li⁺ and Cl⁻ smaller than I⁻.", "2": "F⁻ is small and hard to polarise.", "3": "Cs⁺ is large and polarises weakly, and F⁻ is hard to polarise; CsF is very ionic."}'),
  ('CHE-7111', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.8; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.2', 'The C=C bond energy (614 kJ mol⁻¹) is less than twice the C–C bond energy (2 × 348 kJ mol⁻¹). The reason is that', '["a double bond contains two σ bonds that repel each other", "the C=C bond is longer than the C–C bond", "the π bond, formed by sideways overlap, is weaker than the σ bond", "the carbons in C=C are sp³ hybridised"]', 2, 'A double bond is one σ bond plus one π bond. The sideways overlap of p orbitals in the π bond is less effective than head-on overlap, so the π bond (about 266 kJ mol⁻¹) is weaker than the σ bond.', '{"0": "A double bond has one σ and one π bond.", "1": "C=C (134 pm) is shorter than C–C (154 pm).", "3": "Carbons in C=C are sp²."}'),
  ('CHE-7112', 'Chemical bonding', 'Applications of polarity', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.7', 'cis-1,2-Dichloroethene boils at about 60 °C but trans-1,2-dichloroethene at about 48 °C, although they have the same molar mass. The reason is that', '["the trans isomer forms hydrogen bonds", "the trans isomer contains more chlorine atoms", "the cis isomer has a higher molar mass", "the cis isomer is polar, so it has dipole–dipole forces in addition to London forces; the trans isomer''s bond dipoles cancel"]', 3, 'In the trans isomer the two C–Cl dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel. In the cis isomer they add, giving a net dipole and stronger intermolecular forces.', '{"0": "Neither isomer has H bonded to N, O or F.", "1": "Both have two chlorine atoms.", "2": "They are isomers with the same molar mass."}'),
  ('CHE-7113', 'Chemical bonding', 'Coordinate covalent bond', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.9', 'Which species does NOT contain a coordinate (dative) covalent bond?', '["CH₄", "CO", "H₃O⁺", "NH₄⁺"]', 0, 'In CH₄ each bond is formed by one electron from C and one from H. NH₄⁺ and H₃O⁺ form when NH₃ or H₂O donates a lone pair to H⁺, and in CO the oxygen donates one pair to carbon.', '{"1": "CO contains one dative bond from O to C.", "2": "H₃O⁺ contains a dative bond from O to H⁺.", "3": "NH₄⁺ contains a dative bond from N to H⁺ (all four N–H bonds then become identical)."}'),
  ('CHE-7114', 'Chemical bonding', 'Hybridisation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.3', 'When BF₃ reacts with NH₃ to form F₃B←NH₃, what happens to the boron atom?', '["It stays sp², because boron has only three valence electrons", "It changes from sp to sp²", "It changes from sp³ to sp²", "It changes from sp² (trigonal planar) to sp³ (tetrahedral)"]', 3, 'In BF₃, boron has three bonding pairs (sp², planar) and an empty p orbital. Accepting NH₃''s lone pair gives four electron pairs, so boron becomes sp³ and tetrahedral.', '{"0": "With four electron pairs boron must use four hybrid orbitals.", "1": "BF₃ is sp², not sp.", "2": "Boron goes from three to four electron pairs, not the reverse."}'),
  ('CHE-7115', 'Chemical bonding', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.7', '2-Nitrophenol can be steam-distilled away from 4-nitrophenol, because 2-nitrophenol', '["has a lower molar mass", "forms more intermolecular hydrogen bonds", "forms an intramolecular hydrogen bond, so it has weaker attractions between molecules and is more volatile", "is a stronger acid"]', 2, 'In 2-nitrophenol the –OH and –NO₂ are next to each other and hydrogen-bond within the same molecule. 4-Nitrophenol can only hydrogen-bond between molecules, so it is less volatile.', '{"0": "The isomers have the same molar mass.", "1": "It forms fewer intermolecular hydrogen bonds, not more.", "3": "Acidity does not decide volatility."}'),
  ('CHE-7116', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond length', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 10.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 10.9', 'Which order of carbon–oxygen bond length is correct?', '["CO₂ > CO₃²⁻ > CH₃OH", "CH₃OH > CO₃²⁻ > CO₂", "CH₃OH > CO₂ > CO₃²⁻", "CO₃²⁻ > CH₃OH > CO₂"]', 1, 'Higher bond order means shorter bond. CH₃OH has a C–O single bond (about 143 pm); CO₃²⁻ has three equal bonds of order 1⅓ (about 129 pm) from resonance; CO₂ has C=O double bonds (about 116 pm).', '{"0": "This is the reverse order.", "2": "CO₂ has double bonds, shorter than carbonate''s.", "3": "The carbonate bonds (order 1⅓) are shorter than a single bond."}'),
  ('CHE-7117', 's-block elements', 'Thermal stability of carbonates', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Group 2 Elements; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'The Group 2 carbonates decompose at higher temperatures down the group (MgCO₃ < CaCO₃ < SrCO₃ < BaCO₃). The best explanation is that', '["larger cations have lower charge density, so they polarise and weaken the carbonate ion less", "the metals become less reactive down the group", "larger cations attract the carbonate ion more strongly", "the lattice energy of the carbonates increases down the group"]', 0, 'A small, highly charged cation such as Mg²⁺ distorts the electron cloud of CO₃²⁻, weakening a C–O bond so CO₂ is released more easily. Ba²⁺ is much larger and polarises the anion far less.', '{"1": "The metals become more reactive down the group.", "2": "Larger cations have weaker attraction to the anion and distort it less.", "3": "Lattice energies become less exothermic down the group as the ions get larger."}'),
  ('CHE-7118', 's-block elements', 'Solubility trends in Group 2', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Group 2 Elements; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.4', 'Which pair of statements about Group 2 compounds is correct?', '["Both the hydroxides and the sulphates become less soluble down the group", "Mg(OH)₂ is more soluble than Ba(OH)₂, and MgSO₄ is less soluble than BaSO₄", "Ba(OH)₂ is more soluble than Mg(OH)₂, and BaSO₄ is more soluble than MgSO₄", "Ba(OH)₂ is more soluble than Mg(OH)₂, and BaSO₄ is less soluble than MgSO₄"]', 3, 'The hydroxides become more soluble down the group (Mg(OH)₂ is sparingly soluble, Ba(OH)₂ fairly soluble), while the sulphates become less soluble (MgSO₄ soluble, BaSO₄ almost insoluble, used in barium meals).', '{"0": "Hydroxide solubility rises down the group.", "1": "Both trends are reversed.", "2": "Sulphate solubility falls down the group."}'),
  ('CHE-7119', 's-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 2 elements', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Group 2 Elements; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.4', '0.40 g of calcium reacts completely with cold water. What volume of hydrogen is produced at STP? (Ca = 40)', '["22.4 cm³", "112 cm³", "224 cm³", "448 cm³"]', 2, 'Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂. Ca = 0.40/40 = 0.010 mol, giving 0.010 mol H₂ = 0.010 × 22 400 = 224 cm³.', '{"0": "This is ten times too small.", "1": "This assumes 2 mol Ca give 1 mol H₂.", "3": "This assumes 2 mol H₂ per Ca."}'),
  ('CHE-7120', 's-block elements', 'Anomalous behaviour of beryllium', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Group 2 Elements; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Beryllium resembles aluminium more than it resembles the heavier Group 2 metals. Which property shows this?', '["Its oxide is amphoteric and dissolves in both acids and alkalis", "Its hydroxide is a strong base", "It reacts vigorously with cold water", "Its chloride is a high-melting ionic solid"]', 0, 'Be²⁺ is tiny with a very high charge density, similar to Al³⁺ (diagonal relationship). Like Al₂O₃, BeO and Be(OH)₂ are amphoteric, and BeCl₂ is covalent.', '{"1": "Be(OH)₂ is amphoteric, not strongly basic.", "2": "Beryllium does not react with water even when heated.", "3": "BeCl₂ is covalent, like AlCl₃."}'),
  ('CHE-7121', 's-block elements', 'Thermal decomposition of nitrates', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Group 2 Elements; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.4', 'Which nitrate gives brown fumes when heated strongly?', '["KNO₃", "Mg(NO₃)₂", "NaNO₃", "None of these"]', 1, 'Group 2 nitrates decompose fully: 2Mg(NO₃)₂ → 2MgO + 4NO₂ + O₂, giving brown NO₂. Sodium and potassium nitrates only lose oxygen: 2NaNO₃ → 2NaNO₂ + O₂ (colourless).', '{"0": "KNO₃ also gives only O₂ and a nitrite.", "2": "NaNO₃ gives only O₂ and sodium nitrite.", "3": "Magnesium nitrate does give brown NO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-7122', 's-block elements', 'Thermal decomposition of carbonates', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Group 2 Elements; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.4', '8.4 g of magnesium carbonate is heated to constant mass. What mass of solid remains? (Mg = 24, C = 12, O = 16)', '["2.0 g", "4.0 g", "4.4 g", "5.6 g"]', 1, 'MgCO₃ → MgO + CO₂. MgCO₃ = 8.4/84 = 0.10 mol, giving 0.10 mol MgO = 4.0 g. The 4.4 g lost is CO₂.', '{"0": "This halves the moles of MgO.", "2": "4.4 g is the mass of CO₂ given off, not the residue.", "3": "This uses the molar mass of CaO (56) instead of MgO (40)."}'),
  ('CHE-7123', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidising agents', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Chlorine water is added dropwise to a mixture of KBr and KI solutions covered with a layer of hexane. What colour appears first in the hexane layer?', '["Orange, because bromide is oxidised first", "Colourless, because no reaction occurs", "Pale green, because chlorine dissolves in hexane", "Violet, because iodide is oxidised first"]', 3, 'Iodide is the strongest reducing agent of the halides, so chlorine (and any bromine formed) oxidises I⁻ first. Iodine dissolves in hexane with a violet colour; orange bromine appears only after the iodide is used up.', '{"0": "Bromide is oxidised only after iodide is used up; any Br₂ formed would itself oxidise I⁻.", "1": "Chlorine is a stronger oxidant than both Br₂ and I₂, so it reacts.", "2": "Chlorine reacts with the halide ions rather than staying unreacted."}'),
  ('CHE-7124', 'p-block elements', 'Hydrogen halides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Hydrogen iodide cannot be prepared by heating NaI with concentrated sulphuric acid, although HCl can be made from NaCl this way. This is because', '["HI (I⁻) is a strong reducing agent and reduces the sulphuric acid, being oxidised to I₂", "HI is too weak an acid to be displaced", "NaI does not react with sulphuric acid at all", "HI is more stable than HCl and does not escape as a gas"]', 0, 'I⁻ reduces H₂SO₄ to SO₂, S and even H₂S, and is itself oxidised to iodine, so HI is not obtained. Phosphoric acid, which is not an oxidising agent, is used instead.', '{"1": "HI is the strongest of the hydrogen halide acids.", "2": "NaI reacts, but the HI formed is oxidised.", "3": "HI is the least stable hydrogen halide."}'),
  ('CHE-7125', 'p-block elements', 'Hydrogen halides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'In water, the acid strength of the hydrogen halides increases in the order HF < HCl < HBr < HI. The main reason is that', '["the halide ions become smaller from F⁻ to I⁻", "the bond polarity increases from HF to HI", "the H–X bond enthalpy decreases from HF to HI, so the bond breaks more easily to release H⁺", "the electronegativity of the halogen increases from F to I"]', 2, 'Down the group the halogen is larger, the H–X bond longer and weaker (HF 562, HI 299 kJ mol⁻¹), so HI ionises most easily, even though HF is the most polar bond.', '{"0": "Halide ions get larger down the group.", "1": "Bond polarity decreases from HF to HI.", "3": "Electronegativity decreases from F to I."}'),
  ('CHE-7126', 'p-block elements', 'Oxidation states of nitrogen', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 9.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 9.2', 'In the manufacture of nitric acid, nitrogen in NH₃ ends up in HNO₃. How many electrons are lost per nitrogen atom in this overall change?', '["2", "3", "5", "8"]', 3, 'N goes from −3 in NH₃ (through +2 in NO and +4 in NO₂) to +5 in HNO₃. The change is +5 − (−3) = 8.', '{"0": "This is the oxidation number of N in NO (+2), not the overall change.", "1": "This is only the oxidation number of N in NH₃ (ignoring its sign).", "2": "This is only the final oxidation number of N."}'),
  ('CHE-7127', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Sulphuric acid is made from sulphur by the contact process with an overall yield of 96%. What mass of H₂SO₄ is obtained from 32 kg of sulphur? (S = 32, H₂SO₄ = 98)', '["31 kg", "47 kg", "94 kg", "98 kg"]', 2, 'Each S atom ends up in one H₂SO₄. 32 kg S = 1000 mol, theoretically giving 1000 mol × 98 g = 98 kg. At 96%: 0.96 × 98 = 94 kg.', '{"0": "This applies 96% to the mass of sulphur.", "1": "This halves the theoretical yield.", "3": "This is the theoretical yield (100%)."}'),
  ('CHE-7128', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2', 'Concentrated sulphuric acid removes the elements of water from sucrose: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ → 12C + 11H₂O. What mass of carbon can be obtained from 34.2 g of sucrose? (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ = 342)', '["7.2 g", "12.0 g", "14.4 g", "28.8 g"]', 2, 'Sucrose = 34.2/342 = 0.10 mol. Each mole gives 12 mol C: 1.2 mol × 12 = 14.4 g.', '{"0": "This halves the carbon.", "1": "This is one mole of carbon; 0.10 mol sucrose gives 1.2 mol C.", "3": "This doubles the carbon."}'),
  ('CHE-7129', 'p-block elements', 'Group 14 elements', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.5', 'SiCl₄ fumes in moist air and is rapidly hydrolysed, but CCl₄ does not react with water. The usual explanation is that', '["silicon has empty 3d orbitals that can accept a lone pair from water, but carbon has no d orbitals in its valence shell", "CCl₄ is an ionic compound", "silicon is more electronegative than carbon", "the C–Cl bond is more polar than the Si–Cl bond"]', 0, 'A water molecule can attack the larger Si atom and donate a lone pair into an empty 3d orbital, starting the substitution that ends with SiO₂ (or Si(OH)₄) and HCl. Carbon is small, shielded by four Cl atoms and has no low-energy d orbitals.', '{"1": "CCl₄ is a covalent molecule.", "2": "Carbon is more electronegative than silicon.", "3": "The Si–Cl bond is more polar, which also favours attack at Si."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7130', 'p-block elements', 'Group 14 elements', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 11.5', 'Down Group 14 the +2 oxidation state becomes more stable than +4 (the inert pair effect). Which observation fits this trend?', '["Carbon is most stable in the +2 state", "PbO₂ is a strong oxidising agent, while CO is a reducing agent", "SnCl₄ is more stable than SnCl₂ only because tin is a metal", "CO₂ is a strong oxidising agent, while PbO is a reducing agent"]', 1, 'For lead, +2 is the stable state, so Pb(IV) in PbO₂ readily gains electrons (oxidising agent). For carbon, +4 is the stable state, so C(II) in CO readily loses electrons (reducing agent).', '{"0": "Carbon is most stable in the +4 state.", "2": "This does not describe the trend; tin(IV) and tin(II) are both common, with tin(II) a reducing agent.", "3": "CO₂ is stable (carbon prefers +4), and PbO is stable (lead prefers +2)."}'),
  ('CHE-7131', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of Group 13 elements', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.5', '2.7 g of aluminium reacts completely with excess NaOH solution: 2Al + 2NaOH + 6H₂O → 2Na[Al(OH)₄] + 3H₂. What volume of hydrogen is produced at STP? (Al = 27)', '["1.12 dm³", "2.24 dm³", "3.36 dm³", "6.72 dm³"]', 2, 'Al = 2.7/27 = 0.10 mol. 2 mol Al give 3 mol H₂, so H₂ = 0.15 mol = 0.15 × 22.4 = 3.36 dm³. The reaction shows that aluminium is amphoteric.', '{"0": "This assumes 2 mol Al give 1 mol H₂.", "1": "This assumes a 1 : 1 ratio of Al to H₂.", "3": "This doubles the correct amount."}'),
  ('CHE-7132', 'p-block elements', 'Ammonia', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 11.2; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 1.2', '5.35 g of ammonium chloride is warmed with excess sodium hydroxide solution. What volume of ammonia is released at STP? (NH₄Cl = 53.5)', '["1.12 dm³", "2.24 dm³", "4.48 dm³", "22.4 dm³"]', 1, 'NH₄Cl + NaOH → NaCl + NH₃ + H₂O. NH₄Cl = 5.35/53.5 = 0.10 mol, giving 0.10 mol NH₃ = 2.24 dm³.', '{"0": "This halves the amount of ammonia.", "2": "This doubles the amount of ammonia.", "3": "This is the volume of a whole mole."}'),
  ('CHE-7133', 'Transition elements', 'Coloured ions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'Which pair of ions are both colourless in aqueous solution?', '["Sc³⁺ and Zn²⁺", "Fe³⁺ and Cu⁺", "Ti³⁺ and Sc³⁺", "Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺"]', 0, 'Colour comes from d–d electron transitions, which need a partly filled d subshell. Sc³⁺ is 3d⁰ and Zn²⁺ is 3d¹⁰, so neither can absorb visible light this way.', '{"1": "Fe³⁺ (3d⁵) is yellow-brown in solution.", "2": "Ti³⁺ (3d¹) is purple.", "3": "Cu²⁺ (3d⁹) is blue."}'),
  ('CHE-7134', 'Transition elements', 'Oxidation states', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'In which compound does the transition metal have an oxidation number of +3?', '["K₄[Fe(CN)₆]", "K₂Cr₂O₇", "[Cu(NH₃)₄]SO₄", "K₃[Fe(CN)₆]"]', 3, 'In [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻: x + 6(−1) = −3, so x = +3. In [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ Fe is +2; in [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ Cu is +2 (NH₃ is neutral); in K₂Cr₂O₇ Cr is +6.', '{"0": "Fe is +2 here.", "1": "Cr is +6 here.", "2": "Cu is +2 here."}'),
  ('CHE-7135', 'Transition elements', 'Complexes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'What is the coordination number of iron in the complex ion [Fe(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻, where ethanedioate (oxalate), C₂O₄²⁻, is a bidentate ligand?', '["3", "4", "6", "12"]', 2, 'Each bidentate ethanedioate ion forms two coordinate bonds to the metal, so three of them give 3 × 2 = 6 bonds: coordination number 6 (octahedral).', '{"0": "3 is the number of ligands, not the number of bonds to Fe.", "1": "This ignores the bidentate nature of the ligand.", "3": "Each ligand forms two bonds, not four."}'),
  ('CHE-7136', 'Transition elements', 'Coloured ions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'Why is [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ blue?', '["Water molecules absorb red light", "The 4s electrons absorb blue light", "Copper atoms emit blue light when their electrons fall back to lower levels", "The ligands split the 3d orbitals into two energy levels, and an electron absorbs red-orange light to jump between them; the remaining light looks blue"]', 3, 'In the complex, the five 3d orbitals are no longer equal in energy. Cu²⁺ (3d⁹) has a partly filled d subshell, so an electron can absorb visible light (mainly red-orange) to move to a higher d orbital; we see the complementary colour.', '{"0": "Water is colourless; the absorption is by the copper complex.", "1": "Cu²⁺ has no 4s electrons; the colour comes from the 3d electrons.", "2": "The colour is from light absorbed, not emitted."}'),
  ('CHE-7137', 'Transition elements', 'Electronic structure', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'Iron(II) salts are slowly oxidised to iron(III) in air. One reason Fe³⁺ is relatively stable is that', '["Fe³⁺ has a completely filled 3d subshell", "Fe³⁺ has a half-filled 3d⁵ subshell, which is especially stable", "Fe³⁺ has a smaller charge than Fe²⁺", "Fe³⁺ has a 4s² 3d³ configuration"]', 1, 'Fe is [Ar]3d⁶4s². Fe²⁺ is 3d⁶; removing one more electron gives Fe³⁺ 3d⁵, with one electron in each d orbital, a stable arrangement.', '{"0": "A full 3d subshell would be 3d¹⁰.", "2": "Fe³⁺ has the larger charge.", "3": "Fe³⁺ has lost both 4s electrons: [Ar]3d⁵."}'),
  ('CHE-7138', 'Transition elements', 'Catalysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'Vanadium(V) oxide catalyses 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃. Its action depends mainly on', '["vanadium changing between the +5 and +4 oxidation states as it oxidises SO₂ and is reoxidised by O₂", "V₂O₅ reacting permanently with SO₂", "vanadium having a full 3d subshell", "V₂O₅ shifting the equilibrium towards SO₃"]', 0, 'SO₂ + V₂O₅ → SO₃ + V₂O₄ (V: +5 → +4), then 2V₂O₄ + O₂ → 2V₂O₅ (V: +4 → +5). The catalyst is regenerated; variable oxidation states make this easy.', '{"1": "A catalyst is regenerated, not used up.", "2": "V in V₂O₅ is 3d⁰; the variable oxidation state matters, not a full d subshell.", "3": "A catalyst does not change the position of equilibrium."}'),
  ('CHE-7139', 'Transition elements', 'Complexes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'Pink [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ turns blue when concentrated hydrochloric acid is added, forming [CoCl₄]²⁻. Which change happens?', '["The oxidation number of cobalt changes from +2 to +4", "The coordination number falls from 6 to 4 and the shape changes from octahedral to tetrahedral", "The coordination number rises from 4 to 6", "The shape changes from tetrahedral to octahedral"]', 1, 'Cl⁻ ions are larger than water molecules, so only four fit around Co²⁺. The ion [CoCl₄]²⁻ is tetrahedral; cobalt stays +2 (x − 4 = −2).', '{"0": "Cobalt is +2 in both ions.", "2": "The coordination number falls, not rises.", "3": "The change is octahedral to tetrahedral."}'),
  ('CHE-7140', 'Transition elements', 'Electronic structure', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Transition Metals; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 12.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 12.1', 'Which pair of ions have the same 3d electron configuration? (Sc 21, Ti 22, V 23, Cr 24, Fe 26, Co 27, Ni 28, Cu 29)', '["Ti³⁺ and Sc³⁺", "Fe²⁺ and Co²⁺", "Cu⁺ and Ni²⁺", "V²⁺ and Cr³⁺"]', 3, 'V²⁺: 23 − 2 = 21 electrons, [Ar]3d³. Cr³⁺: 24 − 3 = 21 electrons, [Ar]3d³. Both are 3d³.', '{"0": "Ti³⁺ is 3d¹ but Sc³⁺ is 3d⁰.", "1": "Fe²⁺ is 3d⁶ but Co²⁺ is 3d⁷.", "2": "Cu⁺ is 3d¹⁰ but Ni²⁺ is 3d⁸."}'),
  ('CHE-7141', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Structural isomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.8', 'How many structural isomers of C₄H₁₀O are alcohols?', '["2", "3", "4", "7"]', 2, 'Butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol. (There are also three ethers, making 7 isomers in all.)', '{"0": "This misses the branched-chain alcohols.", "1": "3 is the number of ether isomers.", "3": "7 counts the ethers as well."}'),
  ('CHE-7142', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Structural isomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.8', 'How many structural isomers does hexane, C₆H₁₄, have?', '["3", "4", "5", "6"]', 2, 'Hexane, 2-methylpentane, 3-methylpentane, 2,2-dimethylbutane and 2,3-dimethylbutane.', '{"0": "3 is the number for pentane, C₅H₁₂.", "1": "This misses one of the dimethylbutanes or methylpentanes.", "3": "There is no sixth distinct skeleton; names like 1-methylpentane are just hexane."}'),
  ('CHE-7143', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Optical isomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.4', 'Which alcohol can exist as a pair of optical isomers (enantiomers)?', '["Butan-2-ol", "2-Methylpropan-2-ol", "Propan-2-ol", "Butan-1-ol"]', 0, 'Butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, has a carbon bonded to four different groups (H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅), so it is chiral.', '{"1": "Its C2 has three identical CH₃ groups.", "2": "Its C2 has two identical CH₃ groups.", "3": "No carbon has four different groups."}'),
  ('CHE-7144', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Geometric isomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.4', 'Which compound shows cis–trans (geometric) isomerism?', '["But-1-ene", "1,1-Dichloroethene", "2-Methylpropene", "Pent-2-ene"]', 3, 'Each carbon of the C=C must carry two different groups. In pent-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃, C2 has H and CH₃ and C3 has H and C₂H₅.', '{"0": "C1 carries two H atoms.", "1": "One carbon carries two Cl atoms and the other two H atoms.", "2": "C2 carries two CH₃ groups and C1 two H atoms."}'),
  ('CHE-7145', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Isomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.8', 'Which pair of compounds are NOT isomers of each other?', '["Propanal and propanone", "Propanal and propan-1-ol", "Ethanol and methoxymethane", "Butane and 2-methylpropane"]', 1, 'Isomers have the same molecular formula. Propanal is C₃H₆O but propan-1-ol is C₃H₈O.', '{"0": "Both are C₃H₆O (functional group isomers).", "2": "Both are C₂H₆O (functional group isomers).", "3": "Both are C₄H₁₀ (chain isomers)."}'),
  ('CHE-7146', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Classification of organic compounds', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.2', 'Cyclohexene is best classified as', '["alicyclic, homocyclic and unsaturated", "heterocyclic and saturated", "aromatic and unsaturated", "open-chain and unsaturated"]', 0, 'Its ring contains only carbon atoms (homocyclic), it is not a benzene-type aromatic ring (alicyclic), and it has a C=C double bond (unsaturated).', '{"1": "The ring has no atom other than carbon, and it contains a double bond.", "2": "It has no delocalised aromatic ring.", "3": "It is a closed ring."}'),
  ('CHE-7147', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Stereoisomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.4', 'Which compound can show both geometric and optical isomerism?', '["Pent-2-ene", "2-Chloropropene", "2-Chlorobutane", "4-Chloropent-2-ene"]', 3, 'CH₃CH=CH–CHCl–CH₃: each C=C carbon has two different groups (geometric), and C4 carries H, Cl, CH₃ and CH=CHCH₃ (chiral).', '{"0": "Pent-2-ene has no chiral carbon.", "1": "2-Chloropropene has no chiral carbon, and C1 carries two H atoms.", "2": "2-Chlorobutane is chiral but has no C=C."}'),
  ('CHE-7148', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Isomerism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Basics of Organic Chemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 13.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 13.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.8', 'A hydrocarbon has the formula C₅H₈. It could be', '["only an alkyne", "any of: an alkyne, a cycloalkene or a diene", "only a diene", "only a cycloalkene"]', 1, 'C₅H₈ is CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, with two degrees of unsaturation: two π bonds, or one ring and one π bond. Pent-1-yne, cyclopentene and penta-1,3-diene all fit.', '{"0": "Cyclopentene and dienes also have this formula.", "2": "Alkynes and cycloalkenes also have this formula.", "3": "Alkynes and dienes also have this formula."}'),
  ('CHE-7149', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.4', 'Propane is monochlorinated in UV light. If every hydrogen atom were equally likely to be replaced, what would be the ratio of 1-chloropropane to 2-chloropropane?', '["3 : 1", "1 : 3", "2 : 1", "1 : 1"]', 0, 'Propane has six primary H atoms (on the two CH₃ groups) and two secondary H atoms (on CH₂). On a purely statistical basis the ratio is 6 : 2 = 3 : 1. (In practice more 2-chloropropane forms, because the secondary radical is more stable.)', '{"1": "This reverses the ratio.", "2": "This counts the two end carbons against the middle one.", "3": "This counts carbon positions, not hydrogen atoms."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7150', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.4', 'How many different monochloro products (ignoring stereoisomers) can form when 2-methylbutane reacts with chlorine in UV light?', '["2", "3", "4", "5"]', 2, '(CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₃ has four kinds of H: on the two equivalent CH₃ groups at C1, on C2, on C3 and on C4. So: 1-chloro-2-methylbutane, 2-chloro-2-methylbutane, 2-chloro-3-methylbutane and 1-chloro-3-methylbutane.', '{"0": "This counts only primary and tertiary positions.", "1": "This misses one set of equivalent hydrogens.", "3": "The two CH₃ groups on C2 are equivalent, so there are not five."}'),
  ('CHE-7151', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical mechanism', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.2; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.4', 'When methane is chlorinated in UV light, small amounts of ethane are found in the product. This is evidence that', '["chlorine molecules split into Cl⁺ and Cl⁻ ions", "methane molecules join directly in the initiation step", "the reaction is an electrophilic addition", "methyl radicals form and two of them can combine in a termination step"]', 3, 'Ethane can only arise from CH₃• + •CH₃ → C₂H₆, so methyl radicals must be present. This supports the free radical chain mechanism.', '{"0": "UV light causes homolytic fission into Cl• radicals, not ions.", "1": "Initiation splits Cl₂ into radicals; methane molecules do not join.", "2": "Methane is saturated; this is substitution."}'),
  ('CHE-7152', 'Organic chemistry', 'Electrophilic addition', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.10', 'Propene reacts with HBr to give mainly 2-bromopropane. The best explanation is that', '["H⁺ adds to the end carbon so that the more stable secondary carbocation forms, which then bonds to Br⁻", "the reaction goes through free radicals in the dark", "a primary carbocation is more stable than a secondary one", "Br⁻ attacks the middle carbon first"]', 0, 'In the first step the π electrons attack H⁺. Adding H to C1 gives CH₃CH⁺CH₃, a secondary carbocation stabilised by two alkyl groups (Markovnikov''s rule). Br⁻ then bonds to C2.', '{"1": "Without peroxides, the addition is ionic.", "2": "Secondary carbocations are more stable than primary ones.", "3": "The electrophile H⁺ adds first, not Br⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-7153', 'Organic chemistry', 'Oxidation of alkenes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.7', '2-Methylbut-2-ene, (CH₃)₂C=CHCH₃, is heated with concentrated acidified KMnO₄. The products are', '["2-methylbutane-2,3-diol only", "propanone and ethanoic acid", "propanone and ethanal", "propanoic acid and methanoic acid"]', 1, 'Hot, concentrated KMnO₄ breaks the C=C bond. The (CH₃)₂C= end becomes propanone (a ketone resists further oxidation); the =CHCH₃ end first gives ethanal, which is oxidised further to ethanoic acid.', '{"0": "The diol forms with cold, dilute KMnO₄, not hot concentrated.", "2": "Under these vigorous conditions ethanal is oxidised to ethanoic acid.", "3": "The double bond is between C2 and C3, so the fragments have three and two carbons."}'),
  ('CHE-7154', 'Organic chemistry', 'Preparation of alkenes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.9', 'Butan-2-ol is heated with concentrated sulphuric acid. The main product is', '["but-1-ene", "di-sec-butyl ether", "but-2-ene", "butane"]', 2, 'Dehydration removes OH and an H from a neighbouring carbon. Losing H from C3 gives the more substituted, more stable alkene but-2-ene (Saytzeff rule); but-1-ene is the minor product.', '{"0": "But-1-ene forms, but less of it, because it is less substituted.", "1": "Ethers form at lower temperature with excess alcohol, not as the main product here.", "3": "Dehydration gives an alkene, not an alkane."}'),
  ('CHE-7155', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactions of alkynes', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.18; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.22', 'Propyne is passed into dilute sulphuric acid containing mercury(II) sulphate. The final organic product is', '["propan-2-ol", "propene", "propanal", "propanone"]', 3, 'Water adds by Markovnikov''s rule to give the enol CH₃C(OH)=CH₂, which rearranges at once to the ketone CH₃COCH₃.', '{"0": "The enol tautomerises to a ketone, not an alcohol.", "1": "Hydration adds water; it does not remove a π bond to give an alkene.", "2": "Propanal would need the OH on the end carbon (anti-Markovnikov)."}'),
  ('CHE-7156', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactions of alkynes', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.18; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.22', 'But-2-yne is hydrogenated with one mole of H₂ over a Lindlar catalyst (Pd poisoned with lead salts). The main product is', '["cis-but-2-ene", "but-1-ene", "trans-but-2-ene", "butane"]', 0, 'The poisoned catalyst stops the reaction at the alkene. Both H atoms add from the catalyst surface to the same side of the triple bond, giving the cis alkene.', '{"1": "The triple bond is between C2 and C3, so the double bond stays there.", "2": "The trans alkene forms with sodium in liquid ammonia, not on a metal catalyst.", "3": "Lindlar''s catalyst is too weak to reduce the alkene further."}'),
  ('CHE-7157', 'Organic chemistry', 'Acidity of alkynes', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.17; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.21', 'Which compound gives a precipitate with ammoniacal silver nitrate?', '["But-1-ene", "But-1-yne", "Butane", "But-2-yne"]', 1, 'Only terminal alkynes (≡C–H) are acidic enough to react: CH₃CH₂C≡CH + [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ → CH₃CH₂C≡CAg (white precipitate). The sp carbon holds the C–H electrons tightly, making the H acidic.', '{"0": "Alkene C–H bonds are not acidic enough.", "2": "Alkanes do not react.", "3": "But-2-yne has no hydrogen on a triple-bonded carbon."}'),
  ('CHE-7158', 'Organic chemistry', 'Nitration of benzene', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.12; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.15; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.16', 'In the nitration of benzene with a mixture of concentrated HNO₃ and H₂SO₄, the main role of sulphuric acid is to', '["protonate nitric acid so that it loses water and forms the nitronium ion, NO₂⁺", "remove the nitro group after substitution", "oxidise benzene to phenol before nitration", "act as the electrophile that attacks the ring"]', 0, 'HNO₃ + 2H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + H₃O⁺ + 2HSO₄⁻. The nitronium ion is the strong electrophile that attacks the benzene ring.', '{"1": "The nitro group stays on the ring.", "2": "No oxidation of benzene occurs.", "3": "NO₂⁺, not H₂SO₄, is the electrophile."}'),
  ('CHE-7159', 'Organic chemistry', 'Directing effects', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.17', 'Nitrobenzene is brominated with Br₂ and FeBr₃. The main product is', '["1-bromo-4-nitrobenzene", "bromobenzene and HNO₃", "1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene", "1-bromo-2-nitrobenzene"]', 2, '–NO₂ withdraws electrons from the ring, deactivating it and directing new substituents to the meta (3-) position, where the intermediate is least destabilised.', '{"0": "The 4-position is disfavoured by the nitro group.", "1": "The nitro group is not replaced.", "3": "The 2-position is disfavoured by the nitro group."}'),
  ('CHE-7160', 'Organic chemistry', 'Directing effects', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.17', 'Under the same conditions, which compound is nitrated fastest?', '["Nitrobenzene", "Methylbenzene", "Chlorobenzene", "Benzene"]', 1, 'The methyl group releases electrons into the ring (activating it), so the electrophile NO₂⁺ attacks faster than with benzene. Cl and especially NO₂ withdraw electron density and deactivate the ring.', '{"0": "The nitro group strongly deactivates the ring.", "2": "Chlorine is slightly deactivating, although it directs ortho/para.", "3": "Benzene is slower than methylbenzene, which has an activating group."}'),
  ('CHE-7161', 'Organic chemistry', 'Oxidation of side chains', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.16', 'Which compound gives benzoic acid when heated with alkaline KMnO₄ and then acidified?', '["Benzene", "Cyclohexane", "tert-Butylbenzene", "Ethylbenzene"]', 3, 'Any alkyl side chain with a hydrogen on the carbon attached to the ring (benzylic H) is oxidised down to –COOH. Ethylbenzene loses one carbon and gives C₆H₅COOH.', '{"0": "The benzene ring itself is not oxidised.", "1": "Cyclohexane has no benzene ring.", "2": "The C attached to the ring in tert-butylbenzene has no hydrogen, so it resists oxidation."}'),
  ('CHE-7162', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactions of methylbenzene', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.11; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.16', 'Methylbenzene reacts with chlorine (a) in bright UV light and (b) in the dark with FeCl₃. The main products are', '["(a) and (b) both 3-chloromethylbenzene", "(a) 2- and 4-chloromethylbenzene; (b) (chloromethyl)benzene", "(a) (chloromethyl)benzene; (b) 2- and 4-chloromethylbenzene", "(a) hexachlorocyclohexane; (b) (chloromethyl)benzene"]', 2, 'In UV light, Cl• radicals substitute on the CH₃ side chain (like alkanes). With FeCl₃, the electrophile Cl⁺ substitutes on the ring, at the 2- and 4-positions directed by the methyl group.', '{"0": "Methyl is an ortho/para director, and UV attacks the side chain.", "1": "The conditions are swapped.", "3": "With a methyl side chain, radical substitution on CH₃ is preferred; FeCl₃ gives ring substitution."}'),
  ('CHE-7163', 'Organic chemistry', 'Addition reactions of benzene', 'hard', 'Standard topic not in the PECTAA Chemistry books; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.11; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.14', '7.8 g of benzene reacts completely with chlorine in bright sunlight to give the addition product C₆H₆Cl₆. What mass of product forms? (C = 12, H = 1, Cl = 35.5)', '["11.3 g", "14.7 g", "21.3 g", "29.1 g"]', 3, 'In UV light benzene adds three Cl₂ molecules: C₆H₆ + 3Cl₂ → C₆H₆Cl₆ (M = 291). Benzene = 7.8/78 = 0.10 mol, giving 0.10 × 291 = 29.1 g.', '{"0": "This is the mass of chlorobenzene (substitution, M = 112.5).", "1": "This is the mass of a dichlorobenzene (M = 147).", "2": "This is the mass of chlorine used (0.30 mol Cl₂)."}'),
  ('CHE-7164', 'Organic chemistry', 'Friedel-Crafts reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Aromatic Hydrocarbons; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.13; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 14.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.16; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 14.17', 'Nitrobenzene is sometimes used as a solvent for Friedel–Crafts reactions. This works because nitrobenzene', '["is so strongly deactivated that it does not itself undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation or acylation", "is more reactive than benzene and reacts first", "acts as a Lewis acid catalyst", "is an ortho/para director"]', 0, 'The –NO₂ group pulls electron density out of the ring, so the weak electrophiles in Friedel–Crafts reactions cannot attack it; the reaction happens on the other, more reactive substrate.', '{"1": "Nitrobenzene is far less reactive than benzene.", "2": "AlCl₃ is the Lewis acid catalyst.", "3": "–NO₂ is a meta director."}'),
  ('CHE-7165', 'Alkyl halides', 'SN2 reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.2; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.3', 'Which order shows the relative rates of SN2 reaction of these bromoalkanes with OH⁻?', '["CH₃CH₂Br > CH₃Br > (CH₃)₃CBr > (CH₃)₂CHBr", "CH₃Br > CH₃CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHBr > (CH₃)₃CBr", "All react at the same rate", "(CH₃)₃CBr > (CH₃)₂CHBr > CH₃CH₂Br > CH₃Br"]', 1, 'In SN2 the nucleophile attacks the carbon from the side opposite the Br. Alkyl groups crowd this carbon (steric hindrance), so the rate falls as the number of alkyl groups rises.', '{"0": "Methyl is the least hindered and fastest.", "2": "Steric hindrance makes the rates very different.", "3": "This is roughly the order for SN1, which depends on carbocation stability."}'),
  ('CHE-7166', 'Alkyl halides', 'SN2 reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.3', 'An optically active sample of 2-bromobutane is hydrolysed by OH⁻ under SN2 conditions. The product is', '["butan-2-ol with the same configuration", "a racemic mixture of butan-2-ol", "butan-2-ol with the inverted configuration (one enantiomer)", "but-2-ene only"]', 2, 'In the one-step SN2 mechanism OH⁻ attacks from the back as Br⁻ leaves, turning the molecule inside out like an umbrella in the wind (inversion of configuration).', '{"0": "Back-side attack inverts the configuration.", "1": "Racemisation happens in SN1, through a planar carbocation.", "3": "Aqueous OH⁻ under SN2 conditions gives substitution, not elimination."}'),
  ('CHE-7167', 'Alkyl halides', 'SN1 reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.3', 'An optically active tertiary halide, 3-bromo-3-methylhexane, is hydrolysed in water by the SN1 mechanism. The product is mostly', '["an alkene, because tertiary halides never undergo substitution", "a single enantiomer with the same configuration", "a single enantiomer with inverted configuration", "a racemic mixture, because water can attack the planar carbocation from either side"]', 3, 'In SN1 the C–Br bond breaks first to give a flat (sp²) carbocation. The nucleophile can attack either face with nearly equal chance, giving both enantiomers.', '{"0": "Tertiary halides do substitute by SN1 in water; elimination competes but is not the only path.", "1": "The planar intermediate loses the original configuration.", "2": "Inversion is typical of SN2."}'),
  ('CHE-7168', 'Alkyl halides', 'SN1 reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.3', 'Which compound is hydrolysed fastest by the SN1 mechanism in aqueous ethanol?', '["2-Bromo-2-methylpropane", "Bromomethane", "1-Bromobutane", "2-Chloro-2-methylpropane"]', 0, 'SN1 is fastest for tertiary halides, which give the most stable carbocations, and for the weaker C–Br bond (290 kJ mol⁻¹ against C–Cl 346 kJ mol⁻¹), which breaks more easily in the slow step.', '{"1": "A methyl carbocation is the least stable, so bromomethane reacts by SN2.", "2": "A primary halide forms a very unstable carbocation and reacts by SN2 instead.", "3": "It is tertiary too, but the stronger C–Cl bond ionises more slowly."}'),
  ('CHE-7169', 'Alkyl halides', 'Reactivity of halogenoalkanes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.2', '1-Chlorobutane, 1-bromobutane and 1-iodobutane are warmed with aqueous AgNO₃ in ethanol. The precipitate forms fastest with the iodo compound because', '["the C–I bond is the most polar", "the C–I bond is the weakest, so it breaks most easily", "iodine is the most electronegative halogen", "AgI is the most soluble silver halide"]', 1, 'Hydrolysis rate is controlled by bond enthalpy (C–Cl 346, C–Br 290, C–I 228 kJ mol⁻¹), not by polarity. The weakest bond breaks fastest and releases I⁻, which precipitates as yellow AgI.', '{"0": "C–I is the least polar of the three.", "2": "Iodine is the least electronegative.", "3": "AgI is the least soluble."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7170', 'Alkyl halides', 'SN1 reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.3', '2-Bromo-2-methylpropane is hydrolysed by OH⁻ by the SN1 mechanism. If [OH⁻] is doubled and the concentration of the halide is halved, the rate', '["is quartered", "doubles", "stays the same", "halves"]', 3, 'For SN1, rate = k[RX]; OH⁻ is not in the slow step. Halving [RX] halves the rate, and doubling [OH⁻] has no effect.', '{"0": "Only one factor of ½ applies.", "1": "OH⁻ does not affect an SN1 rate.", "2": "This would be true for SN2 (rate = k[RX][OH⁻])."}'),
  ('CHE-7171', 'Alkyl halides', 'Elimination reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.4', '2-Bromobutane is heated with KOH in ethanol. Which statement is correct?', '["The main product is but-2-ene, formed in a one-step E2 reaction", "The reaction goes through a carbocation, so it is SN1", "The main product is butan-2-ol", "The main product is but-1-ene, because the terminal H is removed fastest"]', 0, 'OH⁻ removes an H from a carbon next to C–Br as Br⁻ leaves, in one step (E2). Removing H from C3 gives the more substituted, more stable but-2-ene as the main product.', '{"1": "A secondary halide with a strong base reacts mainly by E2.", "2": "Ethanolic KOH favours elimination over substitution.", "3": "But-1-ene is the minor product."}'),
  ('CHE-7172', 'Alkyl halides', 'Uses of halogenoalkanes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Halogenoalkanes; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 15.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 15.3', 'Bromoethane is heated with KCN in ethanol, and the product is then boiled with dilute acid. The final organic product is', '["ethanoic acid", "propanoic acid", "ethylamine", "ethanol"]', 1, 'CN⁻ replaces Br to give propanenitrile, CH₃CH₂CN (the chain gains one carbon). Acid hydrolysis converts –CN to –COOH: CH₃CH₂COOH.', '{"0": "The cyanide adds a carbon, so the acid has three carbons.", "2": "Reduction (not hydrolysis) of the nitrile would give an amine, propylamine.", "3": "Substitution by OH⁻ would give ethanol, but here CN⁻ is the nucleophile."}'),
  ('CHE-7173', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Oxidation of alcohols', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.2', 'Which alcohol does NOT turn warm acidified potassium dichromate(VI) from orange to green?', '["Butan-2-ol", "2-Methylpropan-1-ol", "2-Methylpropan-2-ol", "Butan-1-ol"]', 2, 'Oxidation needs a hydrogen on the carbon carrying the OH. 2-Methylpropan-2-ol is tertiary and has none, so Cr₂O₇²⁻ is not reduced to green Cr³⁺.', '{"0": "A secondary alcohol is oxidised to a ketone.", "1": "A primary alcohol is oxidised.", "3": "A primary alcohol is oxidised to an aldehyde and then an acid."}'),
  ('CHE-7174', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Acidity of alcohols and phenols', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.5; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.6', 'Which order of acid strength is correct?', '["water > phenol > ethanol > 4-nitrophenol", "phenol > 4-nitrophenol > ethanol > water", "ethanol > water > phenol > 4-nitrophenol", "4-nitrophenol > phenol > water > ethanol"]', 3, 'Phenoxide is stabilised by delocalising the negative charge into the ring, and an –NO₂ group at the 4-position spreads it further. Ethoxide is destabilised by the electron-releasing ethyl group, so ethanol is slightly weaker than water.', '{"0": "Phenols are much stronger acids than water.", "1": "The –NO₂ group makes 4-nitrophenol stronger than phenol.", "2": "This is the reverse order."}'),
  ('CHE-7175', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Reactions of phenol', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.5', '9.4 g of phenol reacts completely with excess bromine water. What mass of white precipitate forms? (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16, Br = 80)', '["17.3 g", "25.2 g", "33.1 g", "48.0 g"]', 2, 'The –OH group activates the ring so strongly that 2,4,6-tribromophenol (M = 331) forms without a catalyst. Phenol = 9.4/94 = 0.10 mol, giving 0.10 × 331 = 33.1 g.', '{"0": "This is the mass of a monobromophenol (M = 173).", "1": "This is the mass of a dibromophenol (M = 252).", "3": "This is the mass of bromine used (0.30 mol Br₂)."}'),
  ('CHE-7176', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Alcohols and phenols compared', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.6', 'Which reagent reacts with both ethanol and phenol?', '["Bromine water", "Sodium metal", "Aqueous sodium hydroxide", "Neutral iron(III) chloride solution"]', 1, 'Both have an O–H bond that sodium attacks, giving hydrogen gas and sodium ethoxide or phenoxide. Only phenol is acidic enough to react with NaOH, gives a violet colour with FeCl₃, and decolourises bromine water.', '{"0": "Only phenol''s activated ring reacts with bromine water.", "2": "Ethanol is too weak an acid to react with NaOH.", "3": "Only phenol gives the violet colour."}'),
  ('CHE-7177', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Esterification', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.3', 'Ethanol labelled with ¹⁸O is heated with ethanoic acid and a little concentrated H₂SO₄. Where does the ¹⁸O end up?', '["In the ester", "In the water", "In the sulphuric acid", "Half in the ester and half in the water"]', 0, 'In esterification the acid loses its –OH and the alcohol loses only the H of its O–H. So the alcohol''s oxygen links the two parts of the ester: CH₃CO–¹⁸O–C₂H₅.', '{"1": "The water''s oxygen comes from the acid''s –OH.", "2": "H₂SO₄ is only a catalyst.", "3": "The labelled oxygen goes to one place only."}'),
  ('CHE-7178', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Preparation of ethers', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.3', 'Ethanol is heated with concentrated sulphuric acid. Which conditions give mainly ethoxyethane rather than ethene?', '["About 170 °C with excess ethanol and UV light", "About 140 °C with excess ethanol", "Room temperature with a trace of acid", "About 170 °C with excess acid"]', 1, 'At about 140 °C, with ethanol in excess, one ethanol molecule attacks another protonated one and water is lost between two molecules (ether). At about 170 °C, with excess acid, water is lost from within one molecule (ethene).', '{"0": "UV light plays no part; 170 °C favours ethene.", "2": "Too cold for dehydration to occur.", "3": "These conditions give ethene."}'),
  ('CHE-7179', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Iodoform test', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.2', 'Which alcohol gives a yellow precipitate of triiodomethane when warmed with iodine and NaOH?', '["Ethanol", "Methanol", "2-Methylpropan-2-ol", "Propan-1-ol"]', 0, 'The test is positive for alcohols with the CH₃CH(OH)– group, which are oxidised to CH₃CO– compounds. Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) has it.', '{"1": "Methanol has no CH₃ next to the C–OH carbon.", "2": "A tertiary alcohol has no H on the C–OH carbon and cannot be oxidised.", "3": "Propan-1-ol''s C–OH carbon carries an ethyl group, not a methyl."}'),
  ('CHE-7180', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Physical properties of alcohols', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Hydroxy Compounds; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 16.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 16.1', 'Butan-1-ol (b.p. 117 °C) and ethoxyethane (b.p. 35 °C) have the same molar mass. The difference in boiling point is mainly because', '["butan-1-ol is ionic", "ethoxyethane has stronger London forces", "butan-1-ol molecules form hydrogen bonds with each other, but ether molecules cannot, having no O–H", "ethoxyethane is more polar than butan-1-ol"]', 2, 'Both are polar, but only the alcohol has an H on oxygen to hydrogen-bond between molecules. Ethers can accept hydrogen bonds from water but not from each other.', '{"0": "Both are covalent.", "1": "They have similar London forces, being isomers.", "3": "The alcohol is more polar and also hydrogen-bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-7181', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.4', 'Propanone reacts with HCN in the presence of a little NaCN. The product and the first step of the mechanism are', '["2-hydroxypropanenitrile; CN⁻ attacks the carbonyl oxygen", "propanenitrile; CN⁻ replaces the oxygen", "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile; H⁺ attacks the carbonyl oxygen", "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile; CN⁻ attacks the carbonyl carbon"]', 3, 'The nucleophile CN⁻ adds to the electron-poor carbonyl carbon, giving an alkoxide ion that then takes H⁺ from HCN (regenerating CN⁻). The product is (CH₃)₂C(OH)CN. The base (NaCN) supplies the CN⁻ needed.', '{"0": "This would come from ethanal, and CN⁻ attacks carbon, not oxygen.", "1": "The oxygen stays as –OH; it is not replaced.", "2": "Under these base-catalysed conditions CN⁻ attacks first; HCN itself is too weakly acidic to start the reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-7182', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Tests for carbonyl compounds', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.6', 'A compound C₃H₆O gives an orange precipitate with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine but no silver mirror with Tollens'' reagent. It is', '["propanone", "methoxyethene", "propanal", "prop-2-en-1-ol"]', 0, '2,4-DNPH reacts with any aldehyde or ketone (C=O). Tollens'' reagent is reduced only by aldehydes. So the compound is a ketone: propanone, CH₃COCH₃.', '{"1": "This ether has no C=O group.", "2": "Propanal is an aldehyde and would give a silver mirror.", "3": "This alcohol has no C=O group."}'),
  ('CHE-7183', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Reduction of carbonyl compounds', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.5; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.5', 'Butanone is reduced with NaBH₄. The product is', '["butan-1-ol", "butan-2-ol, as a single enantiomer", "butanoic acid", "butan-2-ol, formed as a racemic mixture"]', 3, 'H⁻ from NaBH₄ adds to the planar carbonyl carbon from either face with equal chance, giving equal amounts of the two enantiomers of the chiral alcohol CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃.', '{"0": "Reducing a ketone gives a secondary alcohol.", "1": "The flat C=O can be attacked from both sides equally.", "2": "NaBH₄ reduces; it does not oxidise."}'),
  ('CHE-7184', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Oxidation of aldehydes', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.6', 'When an aldehyde is warmed with Fehling''s solution, the red-brown precipitate formed is', '["copper metal", "copper(I) oxide, Cu₂O", "silver metal", "copper(II) oxide, CuO"]', 1, 'The aldehyde reduces the blue Cu²⁺ complex to Cu⁺, which precipitates as red-brown Cu₂O; the aldehyde is oxidised to a carboxylate.', '{"0": "Copper is reduced only to +1, not to the metal.", "2": "Silver comes from Tollens'' reagent.", "3": "CuO is black and contains Cu²⁺."}'),
  ('CHE-7185', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Iodoform test', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.3', 'Which carbonyl compound does NOT give a yellow precipitate when warmed with iodine and NaOH?', '["Butanone", "Propanone", "Propanal", "Ethanal"]', 2, 'The triiodomethane test needs a CH₃CO– group. Propanal is CH₃CH₂CHO, with no methyl group next to the C=O.', '{"0": "Butanone is CH₃COCH₂CH₃.", "1": "Propanone is CH₃COCH₃.", "3": "Ethanal is CH₃CHO, with CH₃CO–."}'),
  ('CHE-7186', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Acidity of carboxylic acids', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.2', 'Which order of acid strength is correct?', '["ClCH₂COOH > Cl₃CCOOH > CH₃COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH", "CH₃COOH > Cl₃CCOOH > ClCH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH", "Cl₃CCOOH > ClCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH", "CH₃CH₂COOH > CH₃COOH > ClCH₂COOH > Cl₃CCOOH"]', 2, 'Electron-withdrawing Cl atoms spread out the negative charge of the carboxylate ion and stabilise it, so more Cl means a stronger acid. Electron-releasing alkyl groups do the opposite, so propanoic acid is slightly weaker than ethanoic acid.', '{"0": "Three Cl atoms withdraw more electron density than one.", "1": "Chloro-substituted acids are stronger than ethanoic acid.", "3": "This is the reverse order."}'),
  ('CHE-7187', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Reactions of carboxylic acids', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.2', '6.0 g of ethanoic acid reacts with excess sodium hydrogencarbonate. What volume of CO₂ is released at STP? (CH₃COOH = 60)', '["1.12 dm³", "2.24 dm³", "4.48 dm³", "22.4 dm³"]', 1, 'CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂. Acid = 6.0/60 = 0.10 mol, giving 0.10 mol CO₂ = 2.24 dm³. (Phenol is too weak an acid to give this effervescence.)', '{"0": "This assumes 2 mol acid per CO₂, as with Na₂CO₃.", "2": "This doubles the CO₂.", "3": "This is the volume of a whole mole."}'),
  ('CHE-7188', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Hydrolysis of esters', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Ethyl propanoate is boiled with aqueous sodium hydroxide. The products are', '["propanoic acid and sodium ethoxide", "sodium ethanoate and propan-1-ol", "ethanoic acid and propan-1-ol", "sodium propanoate and ethanol"]', 3, 'In CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅ the acid part is propanoate and the alcohol part is ethyl. Alkaline hydrolysis gives the salt of the acid and the alcohol, and it goes to completion.', '{"0": "In aqueous alkali the acid is present as its salt and the alcohol is free.", "1": "This mixes up the acid and alcohol parts of the name.", "2": "The ester is made from propanoic acid and ethanol, not ethanoic acid and propanol."}'),
  ('CHE-7189', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Acyl chlorides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3', 'Ethyl ethanoate is made faster and in better yield from ethanoyl chloride than from ethanoic acid. The reason is that ethanoyl chloride', '["has a more electron-poor carbonyl carbon and a better leaving group (Cl⁻), so the reaction with ethanol is fast and goes to completion", "does not react with water, so wet ethanol can be used", "forms an equilibrium mixture that is easy to separate", "needs concentrated sulphuric acid as a catalyst"]', 0, 'Cl withdraws electrons from the C=O carbon, and Cl⁻ leaves easily, so CH₃COCl + C₂H₅OH → CH₃COOC₂H₅ + HCl is rapid at room temperature and not reversible. Esterification from the acid is slow and reaches an equilibrium.', '{"1": "Ethanoyl chloride reacts violently with water.", "2": "It is the acid route that is an equilibrium.", "3": "No catalyst is needed for the acyl chloride."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-7190', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Esterification', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 18.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 18.3; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 6.2', 'For CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O, Kc = 4.0. If 1.0 mol of ethanoic acid and 1.0 mol of ethanol are allowed to reach equilibrium, how much ester forms?', '["0.33 mol", "0.50 mol", "0.67 mol", "0.80 mol"]', 2, 'Let x mol of ester form: x²/(1 − x)² = 4.0, so x/(1 − x) = 2 and x = 2/3 = 0.67 mol. The volume cancels because there are equal moles on each side.', '{"0": "This is the amount of acid left over, not the ester formed.", "1": "This would need Kc = 1.", "3": "This would need Kc = 16."}'),
  ('CHE-7191', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Identifying carbonyl compounds', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.6', 'Compound X, C₄H₈O, gives a silver mirror with Tollens'' reagent, and on oxidation it gives 2-methylpropanoic acid. X is', '["butanal", "2-methylpropanal", "butanone", "but-3-en-2-ol"]', 1, 'The silver mirror shows an aldehyde. Oxidation keeps the carbon skeleton and turns –CHO into –COOH, so X has the branched skeleton of 2-methylpropanoic acid: (CH₃)₂CHCHO.', '{"0": "Butanal is an aldehyde but gives the straight-chain butanoic acid.", "2": "A ketone does not give a silver mirror.", "3": "An alcohol does not reduce Tollens'' reagent."}'),
  ('CHE-7192', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Carbonyl Compounds and Carboxylic Acids; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 17.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 17.4', 'In acid-catalysed nucleophilic addition to a carbonyl compound, the role of the acid is to', '["protonate the carbonyl oxygen, making the carbonyl carbon more strongly electron-deficient and easier for a weak nucleophile to attack", "provide the nucleophile that attacks the oxygen", "remove a hydrogen from the carbonyl carbon", "convert the aldehyde to a carboxylic acid"]', 0, 'C=O + H⁺ ⇌ C=OH⁺. The positive charge spreads onto carbon, so even weak nucleophiles such as water or alcohols can add.', '{"1": "H⁺ is an electrophile; the nucleophile attacks carbon.", "2": "The acid protonates oxygen, not carbon.", "3": "The acid does not oxidise the compound."}'),
  ('CHE-7193', 'Macromolecules', 'Peptides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 27 Biochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'How many different tripeptides can be made if each tripeptide contains one glycine, one alanine and one serine unit?', '["3", "6", "9", "27"]', 1, 'The three different amino acids can be arranged in 3 × 2 × 1 = 6 orders (for example Gly-Ala-Ser and Ser-Ala-Gly are different, because a peptide has a free –NH₂ end and a free –COOH end).', '{"0": "Order matters, so there are more than 3.", "2": "This would allow repeats of only some units.", "3": "This allows each position to be any of the three, with repeats."}'),
  ('CHE-7194', 'Macromolecules', 'Peptides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 27 Biochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'A polypeptide chain is made from 50 amino acids. How many water molecules are released when it forms?', '["25", "49", "50", "51"]', 1, 'Each peptide bond releases one water molecule, and a chain of n amino acids has n − 1 peptide bonds: 50 − 1 = 49.', '{"0": "This treats each water as joining two pairs.", "2": "A linear chain has one fewer bond than units.", "3": "This adds one instead of subtracting."}'),
  ('CHE-7195', 'Macromolecules', 'Protein structure', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 27 Biochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'Which level of protein structure is held mainly by hydrogen bonds between the C=O and N–H groups of the peptide backbone?', '["Quaternary structure", "Tertiary structure", "Primary structure", "Secondary structure (α-helix and β-pleated sheet)"]', 3, 'The α-helix and β-sheet are held by regular hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups. Tertiary structure depends mostly on interactions between side chains (including disulphide bridges).', '{"0": "Quaternary structure involves several polypeptide chains.", "1": "Tertiary folding is held mainly by side-chain interactions.", "2": "Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, held by peptide bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-7196', 'Macromolecules', 'Denaturation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 27 Biochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'An egg white protein is heated to 80 °C and denatured. Which level of structure is NOT changed?', '["The tertiary structure", "The secondary structure", "The primary structure", "The overall shape of the active or binding site"]', 2, 'Mild heating breaks hydrogen bonds and other weak interactions, so the protein unfolds, but the covalent peptide bonds stay intact: the amino acid sequence remains the same.', '{"0": "Side-chain interactions are disrupted, so folding is lost.", "1": "Hydrogen bonds holding the helices and sheets are broken.", "3": "Shape is lost as the protein unfolds."}'),
  ('CHE-7197', 'Macromolecules', 'Enzymes as biocatalysts', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 27 Biochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.3', 'At high substrate concentration the rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction stops increasing even when more substrate is added. This is because', '["all the enzyme''s active sites are occupied, so the enzyme is working at its maximum rate", "the activation energy has risen", "the substrate has run out", "the enzyme has been denatured by the substrate"]', 0, 'Each enzyme molecule can handle only a certain number of substrate molecules per second. Once every active site is filled (saturation), extra substrate must wait, so the rate levels off at Vmax.', '{"1": "The enzyme still provides the same lower-energy pathway.", "2": "More substrate is being added, so it has not run out.", "3": "Substrate does not denature the enzyme."}'),
  ('CHE-7198', 'Macromolecules', 'Amino acids', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 27 Biochemistry; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 19.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 Chemistry 19.1', 'In a strongly acidic solution (pH 1), glycine exists mainly as', '["H₃N⁺CH₂COO⁻", "H₂NCH₂COO⁻", "H₂NCH₂COOH", "H₃N⁺CH₂COOH"]', 3, 'At low pH the carboxylate group of the zwitterion takes up H⁺, so both groups are protonated and the ion carries a net positive charge.', '{"0": "The zwitterion is the main form near the isoelectric point (about pH 6).", "1": "This anion forms in strongly alkaline solution.", "2": "This neutral form is never the main species in water."}'),
  ('CHE-7199', 'Industrial chemistry', 'Condensation polymers', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 26 Polymers; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 20.3', 'Nylon-6,6 is made from hexanedioic acid and hexane-1,6-diamine. Which statement is correct?', '["It is an addition polymer formed by opening C=C bonds", "Its repeat unit contains six carbon atoms in total", "It is a condensation polymer with amide links, and water is eliminated as each link forms", "It is a polyester with –COO– links"]', 2, 'Each –COOH reacts with an –NH₂ to form –CONH– (an amide link) and lose H₂O. The repeat unit has 12 carbons: six from the diacid and six from the diamine (hence 6,6).', '{"0": "Neither monomer has a C=C bond.", "1": "The repeat unit has 6 + 6 = 12 carbons.", "3": "The links are amide, not ester, groups."}'),
  ('CHE-7200', 'Industrial chemistry', 'Condensation polymers', 'hard', 'Chemistry 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 26 Polymers; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Chemistry 20.3', 'Which pair of monomers can form a polyester?', '["Ethane-1,2-diol and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid", "Ethane-1,2-diol and ethene", "Hexane-1,6-diamine and hexanedioic acid", "Ethene and chloroethene"]', 0, 'A polyester forms from a diol and a dicarboxylic acid, each –OH and –COOH pair making an ester link and releasing water. These two monomers give PET (Terylene).', '{"1": "Ethene has no group that can form an ester link.", "2": "These form a polyamide (nylon-6,6).", "3": "These are alkenes and form an addition copolymer."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

